WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:03.670 --> 00:00:05.910
Welcome to Ear Biscuits, I am Rhett.

00:00:05.910 --> 00:00:07.440
And I am Link,

00:00:07.440 --> 00:00:12.440
this week at our respective
tables of our individual homes.

00:00:12.940 --> 00:00:14.770
Yes, we're still at home.

00:00:14.770 --> 00:00:17.960
I got to stop saying,
yes, we're still at home.

00:00:17.960 --> 00:00:20.850
Hey, we're still at home,
like it's a good thing.

00:00:20.850 --> 00:00:25.130
We are discussing the topic
that we've briefly touched on

00:00:25.130 --> 00:00:29.640
many times while discussing
other things but we've never

00:00:29.640 --> 00:00:34.290
devoted an entire episode
to unpacking this topic

00:00:34.290 --> 00:00:37.570
and it's the business side of things,

00:00:37.570 --> 00:00:41.340
how did Mythical Entertainment
develop as a company?

00:00:41.340 --> 00:00:45.650
Yes, and when we started doing all this,

00:00:45.650 --> 00:00:49.020
we didn't have a business in mind.

00:00:49.020 --> 00:00:50.570
It wasn't like we're starting a business,

00:00:50.570 --> 00:00:54.030
it was, we're gonna create things.

00:00:54.030 --> 00:00:58.560
But slowly over the years,
it's gone from two guys

00:00:58.560 --> 00:01:02.430
making things on YouTube
to two guys leading a team

00:01:02.430 --> 00:01:07.070
of over 80 people, and it's
just definitely a business

00:01:07.070 --> 00:01:09.830
at this point, even though
it was never our intention

00:01:09.830 --> 00:01:11.790
and we never thought ourselves as CEOs.

00:01:11.790 --> 00:01:16.790
So we're gonna unpack that
process and touch on some things

00:01:17.210 --> 00:01:18.740
that we've talked about,
briefly will be getting to

00:01:18.740 --> 00:01:22.960
some new things, but mainly we
want to focus on the lessons

00:01:22.960 --> 00:01:25.830
that we have learned along the way.

00:01:25.830 --> 00:01:29.930
And if you're particularly
interested in business,

00:01:29.930 --> 00:01:32.300
maybe you own a company of
your own or you aspire to

00:01:32.300 --> 00:01:36.110
one day, maybe you can
learn a thing or two

00:01:36.110 --> 00:01:38.343
as we share the things that we've learned.

00:01:39.680 --> 00:01:43.410
Or if you're really not into
that, we think there's still,

00:01:43.410 --> 00:01:45.750
you'd be interested in finding
out the path that we took

00:01:45.750 --> 00:01:47.710
and I think there's applications

00:01:47.710 --> 00:01:48.860
outside of business as well.

00:01:48.860 --> 00:01:51.830
So thanks for coming along
for the ride, no matter what

00:01:51.830 --> 00:01:52.953
your interests are.

00:01:54.070 --> 00:01:57.630
The truth is we never
thought of ourselves as CEOs

00:01:57.630 --> 00:02:01.660
or we never said we wanna
be CEOs, like you said.

00:02:01.660 --> 00:02:05.810
But when you talked about
that, it did remind me of

00:02:06.650 --> 00:02:11.550
when the Fine Brothers made
that video where kids react

00:02:11.550 --> 00:02:16.410
to Rhett and Link and
one kid described you

00:02:16.410 --> 00:02:19.550
as the businessman and
described me as the boy.

00:02:20.459 --> 00:02:21.487
So we became the

00:02:21.487 --> 00:02:22.320
businessman and the boy.
So those are now

00:02:22.320 --> 00:02:24.160
our official titles.
Man and the boy.

00:02:24.160 --> 00:02:26.830
The official titles, like
when you look at like

00:02:26.830 --> 00:02:29.370
a legal document that I have to sign,

00:02:29.370 --> 00:02:31.730
it says Rhett McLaughlin the businessman,

00:02:31.730 --> 00:02:34.653
and Charles Lincoln Neal, the boy.

00:02:38.800 --> 00:02:39.950
And that makes me think,

00:02:39.950 --> 00:02:44.320
when we sign things,
technically I'm president

00:02:45.490 --> 00:02:46.560
and you're--
You have me

00:02:46.560 --> 00:02:47.570
to thank for that.

00:02:47.570 --> 00:02:49.260
And you're what?

00:02:49.260 --> 00:02:50.300
You're not--

00:02:50.300 --> 00:02:51.133
You know why?

00:02:51.133 --> 00:02:52.120
I don't even know--
I'm vice president.

00:02:52.120 --> 00:02:53.890
Vice president--
You know why?

00:02:53.890 --> 00:02:55.840
You have my wife to thank for that

00:02:55.840 --> 00:02:58.110
because I was having a conversation

00:02:58.110 --> 00:03:01.910
with our very first
lawyer and he was like,

00:03:01.910 --> 00:03:04.890
and that was the point in which
I basically, I think maybe

00:03:04.890 --> 00:03:07.120
'cause my dad was a law
professor, I just handled

00:03:07.120 --> 00:03:09.050
all the business interactions.

00:03:09.050 --> 00:03:13.330
I wrote the agreements and--
And I wasn't there.

00:03:13.330 --> 00:03:15.517
You weren't there, and he
was like, "Okay, so just for

00:03:15.517 --> 00:03:19.707
"legal purposes, one of
you needs to be president

00:03:19.707 --> 00:03:21.907
"and one of you needs
to be vice president."

00:03:23.433 --> 00:03:24.660
And he was like, "So
just think about that."

00:03:24.660 --> 00:03:26.130
And I was like, "Okay."

00:03:26.130 --> 00:03:29.910
And so then I'm just telling
Jessie about the meeting

00:03:29.910 --> 00:03:32.497
or whatever, and then I'm like,
"So one of us has gotta be

00:03:32.497 --> 00:03:33.777
"president, one of us
can be vice president

00:03:33.777 --> 00:03:36.240
"and it doesn't mean anything."

00:03:36.240 --> 00:03:39.150
'Cause I think we asked could
we be called presidents?

00:03:39.150 --> 00:03:42.090
But I think he was like
that's a lot more complicated,

00:03:42.090 --> 00:03:42.940
it's not worth it.

00:03:42.940 --> 00:03:45.680
Yeah, and we're like 50 50 owners

00:03:45.680 --> 00:03:47.380
and we have 50 50 interest and we have,

00:03:47.380 --> 00:03:52.127
it's always 50 50 and Jesse was like,

00:03:52.127 --> 00:03:54.537
"Well, since you're the
one making the decision,

00:03:54.537 --> 00:03:57.230
"you should make Link
president because it would be

00:03:58.987 --> 00:04:02.063
"a butthole move to be the
one making the decision

00:04:02.063 --> 00:04:03.700
"and to make yourself president."

00:04:03.700 --> 00:04:07.030
So now, you're the president
of Mythical Entertainment.

00:04:07.030 --> 00:04:07.863
The lesson that I learned is that--

00:04:07.863 --> 00:04:09.960
I'm actually the vice
president and secretary.

00:04:09.960 --> 00:04:11.210
You don't wanna be the president,

00:04:11.210 --> 00:04:14.660
all it means is that I have
to sign more blanks than you

00:04:14.660 --> 00:04:17.190
on things that we still both have to sign.

00:04:17.190 --> 00:04:20.980
Because that was when we created
our first business entity,

00:04:20.980 --> 00:04:25.110
was called RhettandLinKreations,
where the K at the end of

00:04:25.110 --> 00:04:28.160
Link became the beginning
of the word creations,

00:04:28.160 --> 00:04:30.870
if you misspelled it with a K,

00:04:30.870 --> 00:04:34.750
and another lesson learned,
oh, we're full of them,

00:04:34.750 --> 00:04:37.820
is that if you name your
business something people can't

00:04:37.820 --> 00:04:39.420
understand how to immediately spell,

00:04:39.420 --> 00:04:41.690
you're gonna be frustrated a lot.

00:04:41.690 --> 00:04:45.390
It's like Rhett and Link
as one word and creations

00:04:45.390 --> 00:04:48.150
as part of it, but the K is
shared and I know that's not how

00:04:48.150 --> 00:04:50.260
you spell creations.

00:04:50.260 --> 00:04:52.680
I mean we've changed the
name of our company to

00:04:54.080 --> 00:04:55.540
Mythical Entertainment.

00:04:55.540 --> 00:04:57.320
Is it just Mythical now
or it's still technically

00:04:57.320 --> 00:04:58.440
Mythical Entertainment?

00:04:58.440 --> 00:05:00.930
Business name is still Mythical
Entertainment, but yeah.

00:05:00.930 --> 00:05:03.700
And we kept me as the president
so I still got to fill in

00:05:03.700 --> 00:05:05.360
a few more blanks than you, but

00:05:06.504 --> 00:05:09.120
it's just popping circumstance.

00:05:09.120 --> 00:05:11.720
It was actually, it was a
real smart move on my part

00:05:11.720 --> 00:05:13.790
'cause I don't like signing things.

00:05:13.790 --> 00:05:16.850
But the first real lesson
that we learned beyond those

00:05:18.200 --> 00:05:21.240
that I think is what the
operating principle that got us

00:05:21.240 --> 00:05:25.060
off the ground as a
business is we had to find

00:05:25.060 --> 00:05:28.070
the intersection or the
nexus, if you wanna use a word

00:05:28.070 --> 00:05:32.200
like that, of our ability
and our opportunity.

00:05:32.200 --> 00:05:37.200
Yeah, we don't need to rehash
how we got from point A

00:05:37.310 --> 00:05:40.150
to point B to point C,
from being engineers

00:05:40.150 --> 00:05:42.803
to then working for crew and then working,

00:05:43.720 --> 00:05:48.500
hosting a television show
called Online Nation.

00:05:48.500 --> 00:05:50.890
We shared all of that
in episode one of our

00:05:50.890 --> 00:05:53.080
last year's series.

00:05:53.080 --> 00:05:56.131
So you can go back and
watch that if you wanna know

00:05:56.131 --> 00:05:58.730
how we got from point A
to point B to point C.

00:05:58.730 --> 00:06:03.730
But if point C is we're hosting
this show, Online Nation

00:06:04.570 --> 00:06:07.310
and it got canceled, we were
flying out to LA to shoot it

00:06:07.310 --> 00:06:11.380
and then we find ourselves
back in our little basement

00:06:11.380 --> 00:06:13.840
where we were making
videos, back at square one

00:06:13.840 --> 00:06:18.773
making videos again but
we had no income anymore.

00:06:19.940 --> 00:06:21.940
We just had the money that
we made over that show

00:06:21.940 --> 00:06:23.710
and we were living over
that while we figured out

00:06:23.710 --> 00:06:25.500
what it was we were going to do,

00:06:25.500 --> 00:06:29.050
we had to develop an
entire new business plan.

00:06:29.050 --> 00:06:31.610
And there was no, it's important to note,

00:06:31.610 --> 00:06:33.960
no one was making money
on YouTube directly,

00:06:33.960 --> 00:06:35.640
there was no YouTube partner program.

00:06:35.640 --> 00:06:37.640
So you couldn't just
make a video and then see

00:06:37.640 --> 00:06:39.000
money roll in.

00:06:39.000 --> 00:06:41.690
So really, there was no such thing as

00:06:41.690 --> 00:06:42.950
a professional YouTuber.

00:06:42.950 --> 00:06:47.950
So it's a really interesting
idea that we were like,

00:06:48.587 --> 00:06:51.027
"We're going to have a
career," 'cause we were like,

00:06:51.027 --> 00:06:53.260
"Let's make this YouTube
thing a full-time thing,"

00:06:53.260 --> 00:06:55.800
but yet no one was making it a career.

00:06:55.800 --> 00:06:59.497
But again, we were like,
"What two things exist here?

00:06:59.497 --> 00:07:02.007
"We know that we can write
a funny song, we can make

00:07:02.007 --> 00:07:04.560
"a funny music video," we
had had success with that,

00:07:04.560 --> 00:07:06.713
the Facebook song had gone really big,

00:07:07.870 --> 00:07:10.760
and the second piece of the puzzle was,

00:07:10.760 --> 00:07:14.470
is we knew that there were
businesses out there who wanted

00:07:14.470 --> 00:07:15.730
people to know about them.

00:07:15.730 --> 00:07:18.680
So it was like, okay, can we
put those two things together?

00:07:18.680 --> 00:07:22.100
Can we put our ability to write
a funny song with a company

00:07:22.100 --> 00:07:25.903
that wants to advertise
and put that on YouTube?

00:07:26.880 --> 00:07:28.884
And it was really just an
open question at the time.

00:07:28.884 --> 00:07:30.920
And there weren't a lot
of big companies that were

00:07:30.920 --> 00:07:34.160
sponsoring videos, I think
there was some of that,

00:07:34.160 --> 00:07:37.070
it's not like we came
up with the idea out of

00:07:37.070 --> 00:07:40.150
absolutely nothing, out of thin air,

00:07:40.150 --> 00:07:44.320
but there definitely weren't big companies

00:07:44.320 --> 00:07:47.790
investing a lot of money in
this platform where no one

00:07:47.790 --> 00:07:52.790
understood YouTube or
digital video that well,

00:07:53.490 --> 00:07:57.820
but it seemed like our best play because

00:07:57.820 --> 00:08:00.240
the thing that we wanted
to make is the thing that

00:08:00.240 --> 00:08:05.240
we could also sell or at
least sell ad space in.

00:08:05.590 --> 00:08:08.890
And we thought we could
do a good job of making it

00:08:09.940 --> 00:08:14.360
an integration so that
it made sense that the ad

00:08:14.360 --> 00:08:16.480
was a part of it.

00:08:16.480 --> 00:08:19.230
It wasn't like a lot of videos today where

00:08:20.890 --> 00:08:22.730
you will be blogging or
doing whatever you're doing

00:08:22.730 --> 00:08:25.410
in your video and then
you can just insert an ad

00:08:25.410 --> 00:08:28.160
in the middle and people
would understand that.

00:08:28.160 --> 00:08:32.380
And here's a coupon code and here's what,

00:08:32.380 --> 00:08:34.780
here's who my sponsor is kind of thing.

00:08:34.780 --> 00:08:38.240
So we wanted to do something
that was thoughtful,

00:08:38.240 --> 00:08:41.460
that we could be proud
of, but also make money

00:08:41.460 --> 00:08:45.573
and feed our kids 'cause we
already had kids at the time.

00:08:47.310 --> 00:08:50.460
If you wanna really be driven
to succeeded at business,

00:08:50.460 --> 00:08:51.360
have children.

00:08:51.360 --> 00:08:52.390
Have children first, yeah.

00:08:52.390 --> 00:08:54.400
Have children first, that's right.

00:08:54.400 --> 00:08:56.530
'Cause they tend to be
hungry and demanding

00:08:56.530 --> 00:08:58.440
and you come home to them and you're like,

00:08:58.440 --> 00:09:01.830
man, I gotta get after it.

00:09:01.830 --> 00:09:04.350
Well it's one thing to
be a starving artist,

00:09:04.350 --> 00:09:06.140
it's one thing to have a starving family.

00:09:06.140 --> 00:09:10.110
So when you put, other
people are suddenly,

00:09:10.110 --> 00:09:12.030
you're responsible for them,
yeah, that's definitely

00:09:12.030 --> 00:09:12.863
a great motivator.

00:09:12.863 --> 00:09:17.680
So what we did is we
wrote "The Cornhole Song"

00:09:19.210 --> 00:09:23.400
about the classic game,
Bean Bag Toss game,

00:09:23.400 --> 00:09:25.822
that was really popular in
the Midwest at the time.

00:09:25.822 --> 00:09:30.822
♪ Singing cornhole cornhole ♪

00:09:31.280 --> 00:09:34.140
An Olympic sport by 2024,

00:09:34.140 --> 00:09:35.090
we're only four years away.
Yeah, we got two more years

00:09:35.090 --> 00:09:36.123
to find out.

00:09:37.610 --> 00:09:41.450
And we cold called, we
would literally look up

00:09:41.450 --> 00:09:43.440
people who were selling
cornhole equipment and

00:09:43.440 --> 00:09:46.600
we would just find the
number or the contact email

00:09:46.600 --> 00:09:47.532
and we would send a message

00:09:47.532 --> 00:09:48.365
or call them.
'Cause they were

00:09:48.365 --> 00:09:50.000
internet businesses, it made sense.

00:09:50.000 --> 00:09:50.833
They probably will

00:09:50.833 --> 00:09:53.433
understand--
And they were small.

00:09:53.433 --> 00:09:56.280
An internet video because

00:09:56.280 --> 00:09:58.740
they're an internet-based business.

00:09:58.740 --> 00:10:02.730
We weren't gonna call like Coke and say,

00:10:02.730 --> 00:10:03.610
hey, we've got an idea,

00:10:03.610 --> 00:10:05.440
we knew that there was
no way for us to go to

00:10:05.440 --> 00:10:08.820
a Fortune 500 company and
get our foot in the door,

00:10:08.820 --> 00:10:11.040
but maybe we can find somebody
else who is trying to do

00:10:11.040 --> 00:10:11.873
what we were doing.

00:10:11.873 --> 00:10:14.220
And after a few different phone calls,

00:10:14.220 --> 00:10:16.270
it didn't lead somewhere,
we had talked to a mom

00:10:16.270 --> 00:10:18.320
who was selling cornhole
equipment out of her garage

00:10:18.320 --> 00:10:21.488
and she was like, "I
love the idea, but I just

00:10:21.488 --> 00:10:23.680
"can't afford any advertising."

00:10:23.680 --> 00:10:27.653
We finally connected with
the guys at AJJ Cornhole,

00:10:28.740 --> 00:10:32.290
who were about our age,
it was three of them,

00:10:32.290 --> 00:10:34.570
they were starting their business in Ohio.

00:10:34.570 --> 00:10:38.650
They were exactly at the
same place as we were

00:10:38.650 --> 00:10:41.020
and they were trying to just
get it off the ground and do

00:10:41.020 --> 00:10:43.590
something interesting
and they loved the idea

00:10:43.590 --> 00:10:45.820
and they became the
sponsor again, at the time

00:10:45.820 --> 00:10:49.570
I wrote the contract up just
based on some guesswork as,

00:10:49.570 --> 00:10:51.090
like how much do you get paid to do this?

00:10:51.090 --> 00:10:53.370
I don't know, you get some
initial fee and then you get

00:10:53.370 --> 00:10:55.030
some money based on
how well that performs,

00:10:55.030 --> 00:10:56.125
that makes sense to us.

00:10:56.125 --> 00:10:58.970
And it was like they paid
us a few thousand dollars

00:11:00.070 --> 00:11:00.992
and then--
And then we came.

00:11:00.992 --> 00:11:03.170
We knew that CPMs were a thing.

00:11:03.170 --> 00:11:06.130
We knew that there was a
way to get paid based on

00:11:06.130 --> 00:11:08.733
performance because of Rever,

00:11:12.270 --> 00:11:15.160
that website at the time
was actually paying people

00:11:15.160 --> 00:11:17.240
for their videos and their performance,

00:11:17.240 --> 00:11:18.820
which no one was watching
our videos over there.

00:11:18.820 --> 00:11:21.100
But we knew that there
was the concept of this

00:11:22.010 --> 00:11:25.710
cost per million views or
whatever, getting some money back.

00:11:25.710 --> 00:11:29.270
So we did that, we wrote a
song about how iPods die,

00:11:29.270 --> 00:11:31.620
called "The Dead iPod
Song," we asked our friend,

00:11:31.620 --> 00:11:34.710
iJustine to collaborate in the video

00:11:34.710 --> 00:11:36.723
so we could get more views.

00:11:36.723 --> 00:11:39.098
♪ You pressed the power button ♪

00:11:39.098 --> 00:11:41.260
♪ And what happened was nothin' ♪

00:11:41.260 --> 00:11:43.630
I think we even shared,
I don't know if we shared

00:11:43.630 --> 00:11:47.950
some of the integration fee
with her or if she just did it

00:11:47.950 --> 00:11:49.828
as a favor, but it
wasn't that much either--

00:11:49.828 --> 00:11:50.960
Oh, I think we did.

00:11:50.960 --> 00:11:55.950
iRescue.com repairs iPods,
so that was perfect.

00:11:55.950 --> 00:11:57.160
Yeah, they got us off the ground.

00:11:57.160 --> 00:11:58.580
They were the only ones besides ourselves

00:11:58.580 --> 00:11:59.413
who believed in this.

00:11:59.413 --> 00:12:03.910
But then from there, we
got a call to be a part of

00:12:03.910 --> 00:12:05.690
an Alka Seltzer campaign, which was

00:12:05.690 --> 00:12:06.530
a bonafide ad agency.
Well, that was

00:12:06.530 --> 00:12:07.850
a big transition.

00:12:07.850 --> 00:12:10.020
That was a big transition.
I think that was

00:12:10.020 --> 00:12:12.640
the transition from us
having to go out and find

00:12:12.640 --> 00:12:14.690
these people ourselves to doing it the way

00:12:14.690 --> 00:12:17.670
that we've done it ever
since, which is fielding

00:12:17.670 --> 00:12:20.990
the opportunities usually
either directly from a business

00:12:20.990 --> 00:12:22.240
or through an agency.

00:12:22.240 --> 00:12:26.460
And that won in 2008, 21
videos for the Alka Seltzer

00:12:26.460 --> 00:12:29.500
a great American road trip
where we went along the,

00:12:29.500 --> 00:12:32.150
we took an RV trip
across the United States

00:12:32.150 --> 00:12:34.440
and made 21 videos that were food themed,

00:12:34.440 --> 00:12:37.410
and that was the beginning
of us understanding like,

00:12:37.410 --> 00:12:41.320
oh, okay, once you get your
work out there and people see

00:12:41.320 --> 00:12:43.910
that you're incorporating
companies into it,

00:12:43.910 --> 00:12:46.450
maybe the people will come
to us with the integrations

00:12:46.450 --> 00:12:49.750
and that's what happened,
and we basically have

00:12:49.750 --> 00:12:50.930
never looked back.

00:12:50.930 --> 00:12:54.410
I think that nexus that
you're talking about was that

00:12:54.410 --> 00:12:56.870
we were able to do it in
a way that wasn't cringy

00:12:56.870 --> 00:13:01.870
and we were communicating
with our audience at the time.

00:13:02.130 --> 00:13:06.377
We were telling the Mythical
Beasts, "Hey, we wouldn't be

00:13:06.377 --> 00:13:09.537
"able to make this level of
content with this type of

00:13:09.537 --> 00:13:11.657
"production value if we
didn't have the sponsors,

00:13:11.657 --> 00:13:12.830
"so go support them."

00:13:12.830 --> 00:13:16.290
And we're very grateful for the sponsors

00:13:16.290 --> 00:13:18.390
and we conveyed that to the audience.

00:13:18.390 --> 00:13:22.403
And I think that it was,

00:13:23.430 --> 00:13:27.030
it was in no way cringy,
it worked for everybody.

00:13:27.030 --> 00:13:30.460
And so it led to more opportunities.

00:13:30.460 --> 00:13:31.860
Once you start working with agencies,

00:13:31.860 --> 00:13:33.283
that thing starts happening.

00:13:34.120 --> 00:13:35.580
When we started making,

00:13:35.580 --> 00:13:38.300
I mean, Commercial Kings
wouldn't exist if it wasn't

00:13:38.300 --> 00:13:42.070
first for a sponsor, Microbilt.

00:13:42.070 --> 00:13:44.860
The CEO was a fan, he started
communicating with us,

00:13:44.860 --> 00:13:47.120
he sponsored one little
video, it went well.

00:13:47.120 --> 00:13:50.590
So then we hatched the
idea to start making

00:13:51.600 --> 00:13:56.600
intentionally bad local
commercials for his customer base

00:13:56.710 --> 00:13:58.500
who were small businesses.

00:13:58.500 --> 00:14:01.320
And who knew that a few weeks
later you'd be interviewed

00:14:01.320 --> 00:14:05.900
on CNN and talking
about selling furniture?

00:14:05.900 --> 00:14:08.484
The vice president of
RhettandLinKreations on the line.

00:14:10.660 --> 00:14:14.070
So that led to everything,
that led to Commercial Kings,

00:14:14.070 --> 00:14:15.950
that led to us moving to California.

00:14:15.950 --> 00:14:20.900
So again, I think that if you're
out there listening to this

00:14:20.900 --> 00:14:25.530
and you want your particular
skill to be translated

00:14:25.530 --> 00:14:29.030
into a business opportunity,
I do think that,

00:14:29.030 --> 00:14:31.190
and this is not a simple
question, it's a simple thing

00:14:31.190 --> 00:14:33.460
to ask yourself, it's
not a simple thing to do,

00:14:33.460 --> 00:14:37.240
but just do you have
something that people,

00:14:37.240 --> 00:14:39.733
that you can do, that
you're uniquely good at,

00:14:40.610 --> 00:14:42.050
that people are willing to pay for?

00:14:42.050 --> 00:14:45.840
And it sounds almost too
simplistic, but we had

00:14:45.840 --> 00:14:50.590
to start there because we
didn't have a choice except

00:14:50.590 --> 00:14:53.510
to go back and try to use our
engineering degrees again,

00:14:53.510 --> 00:14:56.030
which wasn't something that
we were interested in doing.

00:14:56.030 --> 00:14:58.710
We got some other lessons,
but first, just wanna give

00:14:58.710 --> 00:15:01.480
a quick plug to mythical.com,

00:15:01.480 --> 00:15:03.280
we've got lots of
grooming stuff over there.

00:15:03.280 --> 00:15:05.010
You can groom your lips with lip balm,

00:15:05.010 --> 00:15:07.140
is that considered grooming?

00:15:07.140 --> 00:15:09.110
Technically, and I want
to acknowledge that

00:15:09.110 --> 00:15:13.330
I may not look like the kind
of person who should represent

00:15:13.330 --> 00:15:18.330
grooming as a concept, I
may seem not well-groomed,

00:15:18.740 --> 00:15:21.100
but that does not mean
that our grooming products

00:15:21.100 --> 00:15:22.310
are not top of the line.

00:15:22.310 --> 00:15:25.743
We got pomade, we got
beard oil, we got lip balm.

00:15:26.830 --> 00:15:30.550
And they're all incredibly high quality,

00:15:30.550 --> 00:15:32.900
they smell good, they make you smell good,

00:15:32.900 --> 00:15:34.670
they work effectively.

00:15:34.670 --> 00:15:36.200
Try it out.
And they're all available

00:15:36.200 --> 00:15:37.450
at mythical.com.

00:15:37.450 --> 00:15:40.850
Gifted, rep your boys.

00:15:40.850 --> 00:15:42.480
All right, let's get into the next lesson.

00:15:42.480 --> 00:15:45.310
I think one of the ways
that we tended to approach

00:15:45.310 --> 00:15:49.170
everything, and maybe this
was born out of desperation

00:15:49.170 --> 00:15:52.480
a little bit, was we were
always looking for another

00:15:52.480 --> 00:15:55.690
opportunity that we could see,
is once people started coming

00:15:55.690 --> 00:15:57.940
to us and asking us to do things,

00:15:57.940 --> 00:16:01.370
it was not that we said yes
to everything, but that we had

00:16:01.370 --> 00:16:06.370
this belief that not doing
something won't lead to anything.

00:16:06.820 --> 00:16:09.280
It's doing things that
will lead to things.

00:16:09.280 --> 00:16:12.820
I will be more specific
and say that at the time,

00:16:12.820 --> 00:16:16.030
we didn't know that we've
learned that lesson looking back,

00:16:16.030 --> 00:16:18.230
I don't think we
understood that at the time

00:16:18.230 --> 00:16:21.100
because typically, with
a couple of examples

00:16:21.100 --> 00:16:26.100
that we'll talk about,
we started to do things

00:16:27.180 --> 00:16:28.753
with some intention.

00:16:30.460 --> 00:16:33.400
We thought that something
might come from them

00:16:33.400 --> 00:16:37.190
and it was very rarely the
thing that we intended initially

00:16:37.190 --> 00:16:38.150
that was the result.

00:16:38.150 --> 00:16:41.680
It was almost always just
the decision to do something

00:16:41.680 --> 00:16:43.810
that then led to the
thing that we actually

00:16:43.810 --> 00:16:44.680
ended up doing.

00:16:44.680 --> 00:16:45.607
Sure.

00:16:45.607 --> 00:16:48.580
I mean, we didn't know
that taking the sponsorship

00:16:48.580 --> 00:16:50.750
to make local commercials
would lead to us having

00:16:50.750 --> 00:16:54.710
a television show on IFC
and moving to Los Angeles.

00:16:54.710 --> 00:16:58.660
But we never would have
realized that if we didn't give

00:16:58.660 --> 00:17:02.190
everything we got to those
commercials, some of which

00:17:03.260 --> 00:17:06.600
a few of them sucked, but
a few of them really popped

00:17:06.600 --> 00:17:08.763
and it made all the
difference in the world.

00:17:10.490 --> 00:17:12.843
Good Morning Chia Lincoln
is another great example.

00:17:15.310 --> 00:17:18.640
Good Morning Chia Lincoln
2011, we made the decision

00:17:18.640 --> 00:17:22.490
to start what was a daily
show, the two of us sitting

00:17:22.490 --> 00:17:25.010
at a card table talking
about whatever we had been

00:17:25.010 --> 00:17:27.490
talking about when we came into work.

00:17:27.490 --> 00:17:31.470
We made the decision to do
that simply because we had

00:17:31.470 --> 00:17:35.490
already decided that we
were gonna go and shoot

00:17:35.490 --> 00:17:38.447
Commercial Kings and we
thought, "Well, this might be

00:17:38.447 --> 00:17:41.097
"this opportunity to
make a television show

00:17:41.097 --> 00:17:43.100
"and we're gonna move to California."

00:17:43.100 --> 00:17:44.430
A lot of things were up in
the air, but we knew that

00:17:44.430 --> 00:17:46.210
it was a very real possibility.

00:17:46.210 --> 00:17:49.440
We didn't know what that
was gonna mean for YouTube,

00:17:49.440 --> 00:17:51.330
but one of the things that have
been happening over the past

00:17:51.330 --> 00:17:54.020
couple of years on YouTube that
we had never participated in

00:17:54.020 --> 00:17:58.010
at all was a regular daily video.

00:17:58.010 --> 00:18:01.230
People were doing daily blogs at the time

00:18:01.230 --> 00:18:04.040
and their audiences and
their viewers were increasing

00:18:04.040 --> 00:18:07.010
exponentially because it
seemed like people wanted

00:18:07.010 --> 00:18:08.730
a daily point of contact.

00:18:08.730 --> 00:18:12.240
Now we knew he didn't
want to make daily blogs

00:18:12.240 --> 00:18:14.887
or family blogs at the
time, but we were like,

00:18:14.887 --> 00:18:17.560
"What can we do every single day?"

00:18:17.560 --> 00:18:20.370
And the answer was, Good
Morning Chia Lincoln.

00:18:20.370 --> 00:18:23.300
But we said from the
beginning that it would end

00:18:23.300 --> 00:18:26.040
only as long as a Chia
Lincoln would stay alive,

00:18:26.040 --> 00:18:28.103
which is about 40 days apparently,

00:18:29.600 --> 00:18:31.320
which is how long the show lasted.

00:18:31.320 --> 00:18:32.870
'Cause we knew we'd be
working on the pilot

00:18:32.870 --> 00:18:36.540
to Commercial Kings and also
we didn't want people to get

00:18:36.540 --> 00:18:38.210
attached to it if it wasn't something that

00:18:38.210 --> 00:18:39.430
we were gonna continue forever.

00:18:39.430 --> 00:18:42.000
So we just put an end date
at the beginning knowing that

00:18:42.000 --> 00:18:44.290
if we wanted to continue it, we could.

00:18:44.290 --> 00:18:49.290
Of course we didn't continue
it until after Commercial Kings

00:18:49.590 --> 00:18:51.870
wasn't reordered for a second season.

00:18:51.870 --> 00:18:55.460
We found ourselves living
in Los Angeles, feeling like

00:18:55.460 --> 00:18:59.340
we were back at square one
again, trying to develop

00:18:59.340 --> 00:19:02.580
a new business strategy
and that was going back

00:19:02.580 --> 00:19:06.140
to sponsorships and get
in that role and again

00:19:06.140 --> 00:19:10.697
with music videos, but then
also, developing contact

00:19:12.150 --> 00:19:15.460
with the Mythical Beast and re-instituting

00:19:15.460 --> 00:19:18.060
Good Morning Chia Lincoln
as Good Mythical Morning.

00:19:18.060 --> 00:19:20.560
Again, not knowing what it
would lead to except that

00:19:20.560 --> 00:19:25.560
it would span the gaps in between our

00:19:25.870 --> 00:19:27.480
high production music videos

00:19:27.480 --> 00:19:29.540
where we were actually making money.

00:19:29.540 --> 00:19:32.820
Well, one thing I think we did
know, we had begun to learn

00:19:32.820 --> 00:19:36.700
that lesson a little bit having done

00:19:36.700 --> 00:19:39.340
Good Morning Chia Lincoln and
we knew that people responded

00:19:39.340 --> 00:19:40.840
to it and we knew that we were good at it.

00:19:40.840 --> 00:19:42.320
We knew that we could sit
down and we could talk about

00:19:42.320 --> 00:19:43.190
something for 10 minutes.

00:19:43.190 --> 00:19:45.720
We discovered it by trying it.

00:19:45.720 --> 00:19:47.820
So when you watch that first episode of

00:19:47.820 --> 00:19:51.000
Good Mythical Morning, I
actually saw somebody tweet

00:19:51.000 --> 00:19:53.890
just this little slice of the
video, the very beginning of

00:19:53.890 --> 00:19:57.460
the video, we're talking
about Good Mythical Morning

00:19:57.460 --> 00:20:00.317
in this aspirational way and we're saying,

00:20:00.317 --> 00:20:02.557
"We hope that this is the
beginning of something special,

00:20:02.557 --> 00:20:05.180
"something that you will make
a part of your daily routine."

00:20:05.180 --> 00:20:08.140
And I think the reason
that we were saying that

00:20:08.140 --> 00:20:10.560
is because we actually believed it

00:20:10.560 --> 00:20:12.423
based on this previous experience.

00:20:12.423 --> 00:20:15.430
Then hey, our audience
has connected with this,

00:20:15.430 --> 00:20:18.010
and now we're formalizing it
and saying that we're making

00:20:18.010 --> 00:20:21.450
an indefinite commitment to
doing the show for however long

00:20:21.450 --> 00:20:22.700
and we're still doing it.

00:20:23.580 --> 00:20:26.836
And it was based on that
having just made the decision

00:20:26.836 --> 00:20:28.463
to do Good Morning Chia Lincoln.

00:20:29.519 --> 00:20:33.530
But a huge piece that's
a part of the story is,

00:20:33.530 --> 00:20:36.940
we started doing GMM, but then after doing

00:20:36.940 --> 00:20:41.730
a couple of seasons of GMM,
YouTube came to us with the

00:20:41.730 --> 00:20:45.000
creative innovation program
where they were basically

00:20:45.000 --> 00:20:49.540
giving people money to make things.

00:20:49.540 --> 00:20:51.700
And we had a pretty good
relationship with YouTube

00:20:51.700 --> 00:20:53.020
at the time.

00:20:53.020 --> 00:20:57.330
They said, "Hey, do you
guys have any ideas?"

00:20:57.330 --> 00:21:00.157
And we reacted to that by
saying, "Yeah, we've got an idea

00:21:00.157 --> 00:21:03.260
"to do a variety show
called "The Mythical Show,"

00:21:03.260 --> 00:21:08.260
it's a half hour long show
featuring sketch and music videos

00:21:09.340 --> 00:21:12.150
and weird games and stuff like that,

00:21:12.150 --> 00:21:14.550
taking everything that we
had done thus far on YouTube

00:21:14.550 --> 00:21:16.563
and combining it into one product.

00:21:18.110 --> 00:21:21.360
And at that point, we
of course needed help

00:21:21.360 --> 00:21:24.780
to make the show, so that
was when we hired Stevie,

00:21:24.780 --> 00:21:26.840
who they hired some other people to then

00:21:26.840 --> 00:21:27.673
build the team out for that

00:21:27.673 --> 00:21:29.060
'Cause we had a budget.

00:21:29.060 --> 00:21:31.450
All of a sudden, we were
given a chunk of money

00:21:31.450 --> 00:21:34.450
for the first time upfront to make,

00:21:34.450 --> 00:21:39.040
we were commissioned to make
a show and not just to make

00:21:39.880 --> 00:21:41.940
an integration with the sponsor.

00:21:41.940 --> 00:21:45.230
YouTube was investing in order
to see what they could learn

00:21:45.230 --> 00:21:49.040
from longer form content on the platform.

00:21:49.040 --> 00:21:54.040
So we hired Stevie just
to make those 10 episodes

00:21:55.180 --> 00:21:57.240
and we also hired her to--
12.

00:21:57.240 --> 00:21:58.810
12, and then to build out the team.

00:21:58.810 --> 00:22:01.040
So she immediately turned
around and hired Ben,

00:22:01.040 --> 00:22:04.640
who's still working for us
and he started to build out

00:22:04.640 --> 00:22:05.823
the team from there.

00:22:06.800 --> 00:22:09.580
Well I specifically
remember 12 because just

00:22:09.580 --> 00:22:13.440
this may be of interest to you
if you're in the video world.

00:22:13.440 --> 00:22:15.797
But I remember me and you sat
down 'cause they were like,

00:22:15.797 --> 00:22:18.380
"Well, come up with a budget,
how much money do you need?"

00:22:18.380 --> 00:22:21.070
And we sat down and we
budgeted everything out.

00:22:21.070 --> 00:22:23.370
Now we have been making
videos for nothing.

00:22:23.370 --> 00:22:25.790
So we budgeted everything
out and then we added like

00:22:25.790 --> 00:22:30.790
a 20% buffer and we still
landed at $100,000 total.

00:22:31.570 --> 00:22:32.403
Which may sound like

00:22:32.403 --> 00:22:34.250
a lot of money--
For 12 half hour episodes--

00:22:34.250 --> 00:22:35.710
But that's nothing for

00:22:35.710 --> 00:22:38.730
12 half hour
Is not a lot of money

00:22:38.730 --> 00:22:41.950
as we discovered very
quickly for a half hour

00:22:41.950 --> 00:22:45.240
of what is essentially
television, which at least

00:22:45.240 --> 00:22:48.290
in the first episode,
had a studio audience.

00:22:48.290 --> 00:22:52.350
So we quickly took the
studio audience out,

00:22:52.350 --> 00:22:54.240
we didn't kill them, but
we took them out because

00:22:54.240 --> 00:22:55.570
no one liked it.

00:22:55.570 --> 00:22:59.870
We could've probably
benefited from some advice

00:22:59.870 --> 00:23:02.890
around pricing of that type of thing

00:23:02.890 --> 00:23:03.723
because we had negotiated--
We should have

00:23:03.723 --> 00:23:05.090
consulted someone.

00:23:05.090 --> 00:23:08.010
Yeah, I mean, Stevie as a
producer, we didn't bring her on

00:23:08.010 --> 00:23:11.063
until we had negotiated.

00:23:13.000 --> 00:23:14.620
We came up with a budget.

00:23:14.620 --> 00:23:16.357
Oh, you know what YouTube
was thinking when we said

00:23:16.357 --> 00:23:20.047
$100,000, they were like, "We
could have said $500,000,"

00:23:20.047 --> 00:23:22.690
and they would have probably
still thought it was a deal.

00:23:22.690 --> 00:23:24.820
We just didn't know.

00:23:24.820 --> 00:23:29.140
But we thought just if you
go back, you can still find

00:23:29.140 --> 00:23:30.110
the Mythical Show.

00:23:30.110 --> 00:23:33.640
There's still our hardcore
fans, still like the show

00:23:33.640 --> 00:23:38.640
and see it as a sweet, awesome time

00:23:38.970 --> 00:23:40.223
I know you like it.

00:23:41.250 --> 00:23:45.140
But the reality is that we
thought that we had come up

00:23:45.140 --> 00:23:47.280
with something that wasn't
just a good product.

00:23:47.280 --> 00:23:48.690
We thought that we had
come up with something

00:23:48.690 --> 00:23:50.830
that was going to change
the landscape of YouTube.

00:23:50.830 --> 00:23:54.300
In fact, the tagline of
the show was your half hour

00:23:54.300 --> 00:23:56.330
of not having to click
around on the internet.

00:23:56.330 --> 00:23:59.830
We thought we were gonna change the habits

00:23:59.830 --> 00:24:00.920
of people on YouTube.

00:24:00.920 --> 00:24:04.270
And at the time in
2012, people were making

00:24:04.270 --> 00:24:05.240
three minute videos.

00:24:05.240 --> 00:24:07.090
If you went above three
minutes, it was like,

00:24:07.090 --> 00:24:09.360
ah, this is weird, I don't
know if I can hang on.

00:24:09.360 --> 00:24:11.470
Nowadays people will make 20 minute videos

00:24:11.470 --> 00:24:12.710
and everybody watches them.

00:24:12.710 --> 00:24:14.590
But at the time, everybody
was doing these short videos,

00:24:14.590 --> 00:24:16.500
so we were going against the grain,

00:24:16.500 --> 00:24:19.200
we thought we were gonna
change the way people behaved,

00:24:19.200 --> 00:24:20.760
but it didn't happen.

00:24:20.760 --> 00:24:25.010
No one really cared about the show beyond

00:24:25.010 --> 00:24:26.970
our immediate fan base.

00:24:26.970 --> 00:24:31.970
But again, it's the only
reason that we started growing

00:24:33.530 --> 00:24:35.700
from that point on because
we had to hire Stevie

00:24:35.700 --> 00:24:38.020
who then had to hire a team, and that was

00:24:38.020 --> 00:24:43.020
really the beginning of the
totally new phase of business,

00:24:43.070 --> 00:24:47.140
which was when we began
to delegate responsibility

00:24:47.140 --> 00:24:47.973
and have a team.

00:24:47.973 --> 00:24:51.330
Yeah, I think that's
the big lesson that we

00:24:51.330 --> 00:24:54.960
had to start learning, which
was we had to learn to trust

00:24:54.960 --> 00:24:57.830
others with our vision.

00:24:57.830 --> 00:25:02.830
And with Stevie, with Ben, with hiring,

00:25:04.340 --> 00:25:08.610
we hired Jen to start
helping tweet for us and do

00:25:08.610 --> 00:25:12.240
social media, it's like,
oh, we can't do everything

00:25:12.240 --> 00:25:14.940
any more, and we started
building the team from there.

00:25:16.110 --> 00:25:17.263
Trust was incremental.

00:25:18.510 --> 00:25:21.250
Stevie was gonna do those 12
episodes, but then we started

00:25:21.250 --> 00:25:23.770
to discover that, hey,
we work well together.

00:25:23.770 --> 00:25:26.980
She brings a lot of strengths,
she thinks through things

00:25:26.980 --> 00:25:29.690
in a way that we don't and
she executes things too

00:25:30.650 --> 00:25:32.610
in a way that we couldn't,

00:25:32.610 --> 00:25:36.540
and it frees us up in a way
that we only dreamed about

00:25:36.540 --> 00:25:39.110
or we didn't even give ourselves
permission to dream about.

00:25:39.110 --> 00:25:40.610
That was not an easy thing.

00:25:40.610 --> 00:25:41.443
No.

00:25:41.443 --> 00:25:45.883
And as we've talked to
creators over the years,

00:25:46.850 --> 00:25:49.980
especially back on this
podcast, on Ear Biscuits,

00:25:49.980 --> 00:25:52.470
when we used to interview other creators,

00:25:52.470 --> 00:25:55.730
what we learned is that
this is the common challenge

00:25:55.730 --> 00:25:58.970
for every creator, which
is trusting somebody else.

00:25:58.970 --> 00:26:01.210
It's something as simple as
trusting somebody with an edit.

00:26:01.210 --> 00:26:04.300
If you keep doing it, you're
gonna always be doing it.

00:26:04.300 --> 00:26:07.000
It's you never learn to stop,

00:26:07.000 --> 00:26:09.980
you never learn that someone
else can do it unless you give

00:26:09.980 --> 00:26:13.010
someone else a shot at
doing it and you may have

00:26:13.010 --> 00:26:14.220
to go through a few people.

00:26:14.220 --> 00:26:16.070
We didn't know that, we didn't know that.

00:26:16.070 --> 00:26:18.610
I guess what I'm saying is
I think the Mythical Show

00:26:18.610 --> 00:26:22.090
was the beginning of that
lesson learned because

00:26:22.090 --> 00:26:24.900
for Mythical Show, we
were over the shoulder

00:26:24.900 --> 00:26:28.970
of the editor, we were making
very specific decisions

00:26:28.970 --> 00:26:32.600
about the edit, we put our
heart and soul into that.

00:26:32.600 --> 00:26:35.100
But then by the time we
got to the end of that,

00:26:35.100 --> 00:26:37.347
almost by necessity, we
were like, "This is too much

00:26:37.347 --> 00:26:38.953
"content, we can't do everything

00:26:38.953 --> 00:26:41.010
"and we can't be
responsible for everything."

00:26:41.010 --> 00:26:45.260
And by the way, everything
that Stevie has come in

00:26:45.260 --> 00:26:48.083
and contributed to and overseen,

00:26:49.160 --> 00:26:50.630
she's done an incredible job.

00:26:50.630 --> 00:26:53.100
So then once the Mythical
Show was over and we made

00:26:53.100 --> 00:26:55.800
the transition back into
Good Mythical Morning,

00:26:55.800 --> 00:26:58.970
it was easy for us at that
point to say, you know what,

00:26:58.970 --> 00:27:03.230
I don't think we're going
to actually watch the edit,

00:27:03.230 --> 00:27:04.890
'cause this is a daily show,
I mean this is gonna be

00:27:04.890 --> 00:27:07.380
a big part of your day if
you're gonna be responsible

00:27:07.380 --> 00:27:09.760
for making the show and then sitting down

00:27:09.760 --> 00:27:13.290
and helping to put together
the edit and improve the edit.

00:27:13.290 --> 00:27:17.020
At that point after the
Mythical Show, we set that aside

00:27:17.020 --> 00:27:19.640
and again, we saw the
show continued to grow

00:27:19.640 --> 00:27:23.170
and to get better and to grow
audience without having to be

00:27:23.170 --> 00:27:25.800
super involved in every single aspect.

00:27:25.800 --> 00:27:28.230
Jason, who was working for
us through the Mythical Show

00:27:28.230 --> 00:27:31.083
and then when we restarted
Good Mythical Morning,

00:27:32.770 --> 00:27:34.930
like I said, we were over
his shoulder like you said,

00:27:34.930 --> 00:27:35.873
as he was editing.

00:27:38.630 --> 00:27:40.970
So that was an incremental
level of trust, then

00:27:40.970 --> 00:27:42.880
with Stevie we were able to
build on that, and then you add

00:27:42.880 --> 00:27:45.980
more people to the team and
you start to realize that

00:27:45.980 --> 00:27:48.450
people can do things better than we can.

00:27:48.450 --> 00:27:52.500
And slowly, you move to a
point where you start to,

00:27:52.500 --> 00:27:54.810
we were able to start to
focus on the things that

00:27:54.810 --> 00:27:57.840
we wanted to do and the things
that we wanted to bite off

00:27:57.840 --> 00:28:02.840
and try to figure out, and
it drove us to trust people

00:28:04.200 --> 00:28:07.843
even more because we'd
started to learn that lesson.

00:28:09.000 --> 00:28:13.640
And I think that in some ways,
we're constantly learning

00:28:13.640 --> 00:28:17.930
and applying that lesson of,
what else do we wanna be doing

00:28:17.930 --> 00:28:22.020
with our time that we will be the best at?

00:28:22.020 --> 00:28:25.990
What else are we doing
right now that we can ask

00:28:25.990 --> 00:28:28.290
someone else to do and
give them an opportunity

00:28:28.290 --> 00:28:31.100
to do it better, to delegate?

00:28:31.100 --> 00:28:31.933
So it's--

00:28:31.933 --> 00:28:33.510
Well, we asked ourselves that question.

00:28:33.510 --> 00:28:37.420
I would say one of the biggest exercises,

00:28:37.420 --> 00:28:40.650
the most important exercises
that we do every year,

00:28:40.650 --> 00:28:42.600
we do it multiple times a
year, but there's a time

00:28:42.600 --> 00:28:44.910
every single year where
we sit down and we look at

00:28:44.910 --> 00:28:48.180
everything that we've done,
we look back at our schedule

00:28:48.180 --> 00:28:51.400
and we think, what did we do
this year that we don't have

00:28:51.400 --> 00:28:52.700
to do this year?

00:28:52.700 --> 00:28:54.860
What did we do last year that
we don't have to do this year?

00:28:54.860 --> 00:28:55.693
So it's like--

00:28:55.693 --> 00:28:57.080
And who else can we trust?
At one point it's like

00:28:57.080 --> 00:29:00.080
if I'm sitting there and I'm
like, why am I sketching out

00:29:00.080 --> 00:29:05.080
a concept for a t-shirt when that's not

00:29:05.510 --> 00:29:07.940
what I really wanna be doing right now?

00:29:07.940 --> 00:29:10.380
I can get excited about it--
And we're not good at it.

00:29:10.380 --> 00:29:12.560
Yeah, we're definitely not as good at it

00:29:12.560 --> 00:29:15.410
as Caleb, who's working
for us now and Feldman,

00:29:15.410 --> 00:29:17.900
who's working with him
and that entire team

00:29:18.870 --> 00:29:21.600
with Dana and then,
hey, you got this team,

00:29:21.600 --> 00:29:24.260
you need someone to start
to manage this team.

00:29:24.260 --> 00:29:27.760
So you bring in somebody like
Raz and you start to build

00:29:27.760 --> 00:29:32.760
a team of people who can
give focus and they can also

00:29:33.140 --> 00:29:36.600
pour their passion into it
in a way that we were just

00:29:36.600 --> 00:29:38.260
trying to get it done.

00:29:38.260 --> 00:29:40.690
And if we pour too much passion into it,

00:29:40.690 --> 00:29:43.543
it will be the wrong type of investment.

00:29:44.410 --> 00:29:47.970
Well it's the transition from

00:29:49.900 --> 00:29:54.900
getting people to do exactly
what you want them to do

00:29:55.650 --> 00:29:58.900
to getting them, to inspiring them to do

00:29:58.900 --> 00:30:02.300
what they can actually do
better and that they want to do.

00:30:02.300 --> 00:30:04.410
And that's been a slow
incremental process,

00:30:04.410 --> 00:30:08.480
but now, using Merck as an example, we see

00:30:08.480 --> 00:30:12.700
the t-shirt designs when
you see the t-shirt designs,

00:30:12.700 --> 00:30:15.450
we see them when they're in
the store most of the time.

00:30:16.799 --> 00:30:20.950
And multiple things, it's like
I've got the three different

00:30:20.950 --> 00:30:23.000
Good Mythical Morning mugs right here.

00:30:23.000 --> 00:30:28.000
And again, these were on the
desk and I know you tend to,

00:30:30.410 --> 00:30:32.840
it's more difficult for
you to take yourself

00:30:32.840 --> 00:30:33.673
out of the details.

00:30:33.673 --> 00:30:35.330
But for me, the first
time I saw these mugs

00:30:35.330 --> 00:30:37.760
was when they were on
our desk on the show.

00:30:37.760 --> 00:30:41.250
And I saw them once, and I
gave a limited amount of notes

00:30:41.250 --> 00:30:45.130
but that was a level of
discipline that I've learned.

00:30:45.130 --> 00:30:49.710
And I've also learned to trust them that

00:30:49.710 --> 00:30:52.330
they can design something
that is gonna connect

00:30:52.330 --> 00:30:57.330
with Mythical Beast
even better than I can.

00:30:57.530 --> 00:31:02.530
And that's just something I
had to change my mind about,

00:31:03.220 --> 00:31:05.993
and I think it may seem weird to say that,

00:31:05.993 --> 00:31:09.970
it's like well people are
attracted to what we're creating

00:31:09.970 --> 00:31:13.120
but we just try to be, it's what our,

00:31:13.120 --> 00:31:17.030
I'm proud of what we as a team
create, and I also trust them

00:31:17.030 --> 00:31:19.220
to come up with the stuff
that's gonna connect

00:31:19.220 --> 00:31:23.070
and they prove themselves again and again.

00:31:23.070 --> 00:31:24.580
And that doesn't mean we're not involved.

00:31:24.580 --> 00:31:26.068
I do wanna,

00:31:26.068 --> 00:31:29.480
'cause there is a balance here,

00:31:29.480 --> 00:31:33.330
so the thing is that again,
still using this whole

00:31:33.330 --> 00:31:35.552
merchandise thing and
really you can use anything

00:31:35.552 --> 00:31:38.850
as an example because
we're not making films,

00:31:38.850 --> 00:31:40.520
we're making internet content.

00:31:40.520 --> 00:31:44.510
So if we see something and it
gets out there on the internet

00:31:44.510 --> 00:31:47.120
and then we are like, I
don't like the way that looks

00:31:47.120 --> 00:31:49.700
or I don't like the general
direction that this is going,

00:31:49.700 --> 00:31:51.490
that's actually where
we get involved because

00:31:51.490 --> 00:31:54.440
we are watching a lot of
stuff that we're doing,

00:31:54.440 --> 00:31:56.800
we're analyzing people's reactions to it.

00:31:56.800 --> 00:32:01.220
And so when we think that
there needs to be a shift

00:32:01.220 --> 00:32:04.330
in a certain direction in
any one of these departments,

00:32:04.330 --> 00:32:07.220
we do get involved at that
point knowing that hey,

00:32:07.220 --> 00:32:10.063
you can course correct,
it's not that big of a deal.

00:32:11.130 --> 00:32:12.660
So it's not like we're
completely hands off,

00:32:12.660 --> 00:32:15.410
but the development and the
execution of those things

00:32:15.410 --> 00:32:18.260
on a very minute level
is something that we have

00:32:18.260 --> 00:32:20.420
necessarily removed
ourselves from, and we're

00:32:20.420 --> 00:32:22.992
just managing it from
a higher vantage point.

00:32:22.992 --> 00:32:24.530
I think you're also
touching on the fact that

00:32:24.530 --> 00:32:29.530
we're not just developing
let's say a t-shirt design,

00:32:30.070 --> 00:32:35.070
we're developing a t-shirt
designer who can then embody

00:32:36.870 --> 00:32:41.870
Mythicality from scratch and
Caleb can express himself

00:32:43.730 --> 00:32:48.050
and have fun collaborating with his team

00:32:48.050 --> 00:32:51.350
and with his team leader
and present something to us

00:32:51.350 --> 00:32:53.530
that is surprising and that connects,

00:32:53.530 --> 00:32:57.353
but also sometimes doesn't,
and then that's a lesson,

00:32:58.373 --> 00:33:00.570
you can't tell somebody
something and let them learn,

00:33:00.570 --> 00:33:05.160
you've gotta experience that,
you learn that as parents,

00:33:05.160 --> 00:33:08.700
at some point the kids
gotta jump out of the nest.

00:33:08.700 --> 00:33:10.840
I have a nest in the
backyard, I make my kids jump

00:33:10.840 --> 00:33:13.150
out of it, it's kind of a bird analogy.

00:33:13.150 --> 00:33:14.270
I thought you were
talking about your birds,

00:33:14.270 --> 00:33:16.020
I thought you were talking
about your family of birds

00:33:16.020 --> 00:33:17.210
that you've been raising.

00:33:17.210 --> 00:33:19.610
No, it's my family of humans.

00:33:19.610 --> 00:33:22.540
I've to make them to
learn how to jump out of

00:33:22.540 --> 00:33:25.820
the metaphorical nest, I pushed
them out of a physical nest

00:33:25.820 --> 00:33:27.540
that I've built in the backyard.

00:33:27.540 --> 00:33:30.790
That sounds like a product
that we could sell.

00:33:30.790 --> 00:33:31.623
Tell me nests.
Think about

00:33:31.623 --> 00:33:32.840
Mythical Kitchen.

00:33:32.840 --> 00:33:36.490
I think that's another
very current example

00:33:36.490 --> 00:33:40.870
of that team

00:33:40.870 --> 00:33:44.570
developing content
within certain parameters

00:33:44.570 --> 00:33:48.780
and vision that we've helped
set up that in order for

00:33:48.780 --> 00:33:53.780
Mythical Kitchen to really
develop a life of its own,

00:33:54.190 --> 00:33:59.070
it needs to be an expression
of Josh in that team

00:33:59.070 --> 00:34:02.040
working together to passionately
put stuff out into the

00:34:02.040 --> 00:34:04.830
world to recreate the
experience that we've had

00:34:04.830 --> 00:34:08.060
and we'll continue to have on the content

00:34:08.060 --> 00:34:10.193
that we're directly working on.

00:34:12.233 --> 00:34:15.900
Not only do they know the kind of content

00:34:15.900 --> 00:34:19.150
that would work from a culinary
entertainment standpoint

00:34:19.150 --> 00:34:21.770
better than we do because
that's what Josh would have

00:34:21.770 --> 00:34:26.770
lives and breathes, they're
also better at executing it.

00:34:26.830 --> 00:34:31.830
I think that our role
in that was just knowing

00:34:32.170 --> 00:34:36.920
that we wanted to develop our
culinary team to the place

00:34:36.920 --> 00:34:41.650
where they're not just making food for

00:34:41.650 --> 00:34:43.790
Good Mythical Morning, but they are also,

00:34:43.790 --> 00:34:46.480
this is an opportunity
to create content that

00:34:46.480 --> 00:34:49.770
a whole new fan or also
existing fans could connect with

00:34:49.770 --> 00:34:54.190
because people like this
kind of food-based content.

00:34:54.190 --> 00:34:58.810
And then helping to empower
Josh to see his potential as

00:34:58.810 --> 00:35:01.180
somebody who could have his own audience.

00:35:01.180 --> 00:35:05.560
That was pretty much the
vision, the nebulous vision

00:35:05.560 --> 00:35:06.640
that we had.

00:35:06.640 --> 00:35:09.170
But then the specific execution
of well, what is gonna be

00:35:09.170 --> 00:35:11.330
on the channel and what
the nature of the video

00:35:11.330 --> 00:35:14.850
is going to be, that's
the Mythical Kitchen team

00:35:16.820 --> 00:35:21.050
with the help of Stevie
leading things creatively

00:35:21.050 --> 00:35:23.360
and then working together
as a creative team,

00:35:23.360 --> 00:35:27.430
that is something that we're
just shepherding loosely

00:35:28.290 --> 00:35:31.810
and it's a better product because of that.

00:35:31.810 --> 00:35:33.110
Because as soon as,

00:35:33.110 --> 00:35:35.330
I mean, have you spent
a bunch of time watching

00:35:35.330 --> 00:35:37.390
recipe videos on the internet?

00:35:37.390 --> 00:35:38.260
No, have I?

00:35:38.260 --> 00:35:39.093
No.

00:35:39.093 --> 00:35:41.880
Maybe more than you have, but not enough,

00:35:41.880 --> 00:35:43.703
it's not like I'm an expert on that.

00:35:44.600 --> 00:35:49.190
But now we can see what the
content is and we can offer

00:35:49.190 --> 00:35:52.600
some feedback, but again
we don't have the time

00:35:52.600 --> 00:35:56.360
or the ability, it wouldn't
happen if we insisted on being

00:35:56.360 --> 00:35:59.660
the ones who were involved,
it would never happen.

00:35:59.660 --> 00:36:00.940
And if it did happen,
it wouldn't be as good

00:36:00.940 --> 00:36:01.773
as it is right now.

00:36:01.773 --> 00:36:03.960
I think another shift that
we've made, just to move to

00:36:03.960 --> 00:36:08.140
a new lesson learned,
is shifting from being

00:36:09.220 --> 00:36:12.703
solely reactive to being proactive.

00:36:14.720 --> 00:36:17.600
I guess it's not saying much
to say if you're running

00:36:17.600 --> 00:36:21.850
a company, you should have
a plan and you should think

00:36:21.850 --> 00:36:23.003
into the future.

00:36:23.980 --> 00:36:27.780
It's been extremely difficult
as you're walking on the

00:36:27.780 --> 00:36:32.040
sidewalk of internetainment,
you can see it forming

00:36:32.040 --> 00:36:34.193
right beneath your toes.

00:36:35.230 --> 00:36:38.250
There's not a lot of people
that have gone before us

00:36:38.250 --> 00:36:40.650
that we can emulate.

00:36:40.650 --> 00:36:45.650
So it has been difficult
to take a longterm approach

00:36:45.950 --> 00:36:50.070
or to have that proactive
stance when it's like,

00:36:50.070 --> 00:36:51.940
I don't know what the next opportunity is

00:36:51.940 --> 00:36:54.300
and we just need to say
yes because saying no

00:36:54.300 --> 00:36:55.990
leads to nothing.

00:36:55.990 --> 00:36:58.150
It's like well, at a
certain point, we got it,

00:36:58.150 --> 00:37:00.780
we got to think, more forward.

00:37:00.780 --> 00:37:04.010
And as difficult as it is
to develop a three year

00:37:04.010 --> 00:37:07.470
or five year plan, we are
getting better at laying out

00:37:07.470 --> 00:37:10.900
our longterm vision, knowing
that it's gotta be flexible

00:37:10.900 --> 00:37:12.530
given the environment that we're in.

00:37:12.530 --> 00:37:17.120
But I think an example of
this that crystallizes it down

00:37:17.120 --> 00:37:21.860
is the way that we have grown
in terms of the physical space

00:37:21.860 --> 00:37:23.920
that we're in as a team.

00:37:23.920 --> 00:37:26.970
So going from, of course
we had the basement

00:37:26.970 --> 00:37:29.410
and then the studio and
Fuquay but moving out

00:37:29.410 --> 00:37:32.680
to California where all of a
sudden the mortgage on my home

00:37:32.680 --> 00:37:37.680
was, or the rent on my home was

00:37:38.490 --> 00:37:40.740
five times what the mortgage

00:37:40.740 --> 00:37:44.623
on my home in North
Carolina was, or six times,

00:37:45.750 --> 00:37:47.880
things were suddenly very
different and it wasn't like,

00:37:47.880 --> 00:37:50.980
oh, I can pay this rent and
also we can have a studio.

00:37:50.980 --> 00:37:53.020
Like no, we weren't making
enough money for that.

00:37:53.020 --> 00:37:57.770
So we thankfully had a garage
that we were in at the time

00:37:57.770 --> 00:38:01.500
and of course it was the two
of us and Jason in there,

00:38:01.500 --> 00:38:03.823
and then even the first
meeting that we had with Stevie

00:38:03.823 --> 00:38:06.120
at her first day of work,
she was in there with us

00:38:06.120 --> 00:38:08.190
and we were very quickly
realizing that this is not gonna

00:38:08.190 --> 00:38:11.975
cut it, we gotta have a space,
but we're so conservative

00:38:11.975 --> 00:38:16.040
in the way that we estimated
what we're gonna have to pay

00:38:16.040 --> 00:38:20.320
and we were operating,
I would say fear based.

00:38:20.320 --> 00:38:23.010
It was practical, but
it was a little bit like

00:38:23.010 --> 00:38:24.910
don't bite off more than you can chew.

00:38:25.800 --> 00:38:28.240
So do you remember that
first time that we went out

00:38:28.240 --> 00:38:31.770
and looked at different spaces in Burbank

00:38:31.770 --> 00:38:34.183
before we found the first spot?

00:38:35.720 --> 00:38:40.233
Well, I specifically remember
looking at that place,

00:38:41.623 --> 00:38:44.773
I don't know what street
it's on, but it's like

00:38:45.850 --> 00:38:49.390
a storefront, it has like a glass front,

00:38:49.390 --> 00:38:52.920
it looks like a hair salon and
it's on a major thoroughfare

00:38:53.810 --> 00:38:55.940
that people could just
walk in, which incidentally

00:38:55.940 --> 00:38:58.860
is what we had in Fuquay, but
Fuquay is not Los Angeles.

00:38:58.860 --> 00:39:02.610
And I remember seriously
considering that and of course

00:39:02.610 --> 00:39:04.840
what we ended up going with
was a very nondescript place

00:39:04.840 --> 00:39:06.840
that no one would ever,
even though people did knock

00:39:06.840 --> 00:39:08.730
on the door every once in
a while, I don't even know

00:39:08.730 --> 00:39:10.080
what that place was, but--

00:39:10.080 --> 00:39:12.480
Downstairs is where we had
a room to build a set for

00:39:12.480 --> 00:39:13.460
Good Mythical Morning.

00:39:13.460 --> 00:39:16.580
Set is a very loose term there.

00:39:16.580 --> 00:39:19.820
It was a dedicated place
where we could go downstairs

00:39:19.820 --> 00:39:22.940
and film episodes of the show
and then we could go back

00:39:22.940 --> 00:39:25.810
upstairs and just work at our
desks, 'cause it's not like

00:39:25.810 --> 00:39:29.800
we had to turn the camera
in and it's not like we had

00:39:29.800 --> 00:39:31.780
to set up the camera
so that our desks were

00:39:31.780 --> 00:39:34.303
in the background, this
was definitely a step up.

00:39:34.303 --> 00:39:37.250
And we got to a place
within a few years of being

00:39:37.250 --> 00:39:42.250
in that spot where everyone
was working pretty much

00:39:43.010 --> 00:39:45.040
on top of one another.

00:39:45.040 --> 00:39:48.240
We had not literally stacked
desks on top of each other,

00:39:48.240 --> 00:39:52.120
but that was the only
logical place to go next.

00:39:52.120 --> 00:39:55.490
And so we were like, "Okay,
let's look for a new space."

00:39:55.490 --> 00:39:58.230
And do you remember the place that we were

00:39:58.230 --> 00:39:59.870
very close to renting?

00:39:59.870 --> 00:40:02.460
Yeah, the guitarist for the doors,

00:40:02.460 --> 00:40:06.830
the Jim Morrison band, the
doors was renting a storefront.

00:40:06.830 --> 00:40:09.460
We never met him, but the
agent showed us around

00:40:09.460 --> 00:40:14.460
and in the back, it was
just like a cement slab

00:40:14.870 --> 00:40:17.487
and it was on air
conditioned, but we were like,

00:40:17.487 --> 00:40:20.290
"Well, we can build two sets back here."

00:40:20.290 --> 00:40:22.600
I mean it's on air
conditioning so it's gonna get

00:40:22.600 --> 00:40:24.265
to like 110 degrees.

00:40:24.265 --> 00:40:26.980
It was very warehousing.

00:40:26.980 --> 00:40:31.180
It had a giant beam that went through,

00:40:31.180 --> 00:40:33.070
there was a giant beam
that went through it,

00:40:33.070 --> 00:40:34.850
remember there was this
conversation about what we could do

00:40:34.850 --> 00:40:37.840
with that giant beam
'cause it was gonna be

00:40:37.840 --> 00:40:38.890
in all the shots, it was--

00:40:38.890 --> 00:40:40.116
It was just storage.

00:40:40.116 --> 00:40:43.140
They were shipping
stuff, they had shelving,

00:40:43.140 --> 00:40:46.020
it would've been horrible,
it would've been a downgrade,

00:40:46.020 --> 00:40:48.580
but we were seriously considering it.

00:40:48.580 --> 00:40:52.630
But we were, no, we weren't
just seriously considering it,

00:40:52.630 --> 00:40:55.247
we got down to the place where we said,

00:40:55.247 --> 00:40:56.837
"If this guy is willing to do this,

00:40:56.837 --> 00:41:00.337
"if the landlord is willing
to do this one alteration,

00:41:00.337 --> 00:41:01.430
"then we'll rent it."

00:41:01.430 --> 00:41:03.540
In the meantime, while we
were waiting on an answer back

00:41:03.540 --> 00:41:08.060
for that, we went to the
studio that we're currently in

00:41:08.060 --> 00:41:10.660
and now this was a legitimate studio.

00:41:10.660 --> 00:41:14.430
It had a real set with soundproofing,

00:41:14.430 --> 00:41:16.930
television shows had
been shot on this set.

00:41:16.930 --> 00:41:21.320
It had a recording studio,
it had all these things

00:41:21.320 --> 00:41:26.320
that we needed and wanted,
but we were looking at it not,

00:41:27.740 --> 00:41:29.720
we weren't really looking
at it to consider it,

00:41:29.720 --> 00:41:33.057
we were looking at it
because our realtor like,

00:41:33.057 --> 00:41:35.897
"Here's another spot, this is
way out of your price range,

00:41:35.897 --> 00:41:37.547
"but you should look at it just and see

00:41:37.547 --> 00:41:39.320
"that what's available."

00:41:39.320 --> 00:41:42.670
And I remember walking around
our space and thinking,

00:41:42.670 --> 00:41:47.670
man, this is awesome, but I can't imagine,

00:41:48.590 --> 00:41:51.370
I can't imagine filling
this place up with people,

00:41:51.370 --> 00:41:52.760
I can't imagine getting to a,

00:41:52.760 --> 00:41:55.440
how could we ever get to a
place where we would have

00:41:55.440 --> 00:41:57.930
enough people to work in this place?

00:41:57.930 --> 00:42:00.460
And then I think me and
you were on the same page,

00:42:00.460 --> 00:42:04.230
which was we can't afford
this and it's too big,

00:42:04.230 --> 00:42:06.750
and then Ben, who was
working for us at the time,

00:42:06.750 --> 00:42:09.567
I remember he pulled us
aside and he was like,

00:42:09.567 --> 00:42:13.260
"Guys, I know that the
place that we looked at,"

00:42:13.260 --> 00:42:15.450
'cause everybody, we all went
together to look at the places

00:42:15.450 --> 00:42:17.877
'cause it was our very small team.

00:42:17.877 --> 00:42:21.347
"I know that the place
that we looked at yesterday

00:42:21.347 --> 00:42:26.347
"is like the Ferrari of
studios, at least in our minds."

00:42:28.647 --> 00:42:30.807
"And I know that it seems
like it might be too much,

00:42:30.807 --> 00:42:33.330
"but I just wanna say that,"

00:42:33.330 --> 00:42:35.800
I can't remember his exact
words, but it was essentially,

00:42:35.800 --> 00:42:38.860
I know that we could use it
and I believe that we could

00:42:39.750 --> 00:42:41.580
do great things there.

00:42:41.580 --> 00:42:42.670
And I just want to,

00:42:42.670 --> 00:42:46.853
almost from a technical perspective
as your cinematographer,

00:42:48.330 --> 00:42:49.940
the guy who makes things
look a certain way,

00:42:49.940 --> 00:42:53.400
I just want to let you know
that this would be the real deal

00:42:54.290 --> 00:42:56.654
and we ended up doing it.
And it made a difference.

00:42:56.654 --> 00:42:58.451
And he was right.

00:42:58.451 --> 00:43:02.060
We ended up, when the other
side of the building came up

00:43:03.366 --> 00:43:05.940
for lease, we had a different mentality.

00:43:05.940 --> 00:43:10.007
We were like, "Okay when
would there be an opportunity

00:43:10.007 --> 00:43:15.007
"to rent the contiguous space
and double our capacity,

00:43:16.737 --> 00:43:19.820
"it will not happen again if
somebody else scoops this up."

00:43:19.820 --> 00:43:21.930
So it was like that really worked out.

00:43:21.930 --> 00:43:23.760
Well, we didn't have an idea at the,

00:43:23.760 --> 00:43:27.110
again by that time like you
said, our mentality had changed.

00:43:27.110 --> 00:43:32.010
We got into the space, we
used it, we quickly got to

00:43:32.010 --> 00:43:34.940
capacity there 'cause that
seems to be what always happens.

00:43:34.940 --> 00:43:37.300
And then when that space
next door became available,

00:43:37.300 --> 00:43:39.410
we didn't have any plans for expansion,

00:43:39.410 --> 00:43:42.630
we didn't have any
specific ideas to fill it,

00:43:42.630 --> 00:43:45.450
but it was one of the first
things that we've done

00:43:45.450 --> 00:43:48.680
that was a bigger risk
that was very proactive.

00:43:48.680 --> 00:43:50.420
And it wasn't waiting for
somebody to come along

00:43:50.420 --> 00:43:52.483
with an idea or an opportunity
that we can then execute

00:43:52.483 --> 00:43:55.310
in that space, it was very
much, let's get the space

00:43:55.310 --> 00:43:57.170
and let's expect the opportunity.

00:43:57.170 --> 00:44:00.720
And lo and behold, right
after we started renting it,

00:44:00.720 --> 00:44:03.600
like literally months after we
made the decision to rent it,

00:44:03.600 --> 00:44:05.927
YouTube came along and said,
"Hey, we wanna fund a bigger

00:44:05.927 --> 00:44:08.150
"version of GMM," and that
was when all of a sudden

00:44:08.150 --> 00:44:12.530
we had to hire like 50
people and we had to put them

00:44:12.530 --> 00:44:16.310
somewhere and lo and behold,
we had the space next door.

00:44:16.310 --> 00:44:20.653
And just to move on to the next lesson,

00:44:21.740 --> 00:44:26.370
it's interesting how our
culture evolved and how

00:44:26.370 --> 00:44:30.217
our understanding of what
it meant to be leaders

00:44:32.510 --> 00:44:37.510
in a growing company and
how to tackle the challenges

00:44:37.670 --> 00:44:40.480
of actually providing leadership and how

00:44:45.700 --> 00:44:50.060
to give our vision to our
employees and then empower them

00:44:50.060 --> 00:44:53.010
and then just shape a culture
where we could continue

00:44:53.010 --> 00:44:58.010
to work together and achieve
our goals and also the ideas

00:44:58.530 --> 00:45:00.500
that they could come up with.

00:45:00.500 --> 00:45:03.280
There was a lot of adjustments
in there that we really

00:45:03.280 --> 00:45:04.940
started to come to grips with.

00:45:04.940 --> 00:45:07.518
We are running a business
here and I think a lot of

00:45:07.518 --> 00:45:09.490
the culture.

00:45:09.490 --> 00:45:13.120
The lesson very specifically
is that we learned

00:45:13.120 --> 00:45:14.393
that we had a culture.

00:45:16.111 --> 00:45:18.100
That wasn't something that
we had ever thought about.

00:45:18.100 --> 00:45:22.440
We had been two guys who
were executing a vision,

00:45:22.440 --> 00:45:25.390
trying to create things and
hiring people to contribute

00:45:25.390 --> 00:45:29.130
directly to that and
empowering them and delegating.

00:45:29.130 --> 00:45:34.130
But it wasn't until we
got to about 20 people

00:45:34.550 --> 00:45:36.100
when it started to really hit us.

00:45:36.100 --> 00:45:39.573
I remember, we were
still doing things like,

00:45:40.490 --> 00:45:44.357
still doing the books ourselves and just

00:45:47.850 --> 00:45:50.810
it was like we were unwilling
to admit that we had

00:45:50.810 --> 00:45:53.780
a business, it was a small business,

00:45:53.780 --> 00:45:58.250
but we had a small business
and that intentionally

00:45:58.250 --> 00:46:01.750
or otherwise, we had
established a certain culture

00:46:01.750 --> 00:46:03.763
and people could read it,
but it wasn't something

00:46:03.763 --> 00:46:05.210
that we were doing on
purpose, it was something

00:46:05.210 --> 00:46:07.810
that was just happening
based on our personnel.

00:46:07.810 --> 00:46:11.720
And I think when we hired
Brian, a very strategic hire

00:46:11.720 --> 00:46:15.380
for us as our chief operating officer

00:46:15.380 --> 00:46:19.080
and head of business
development, he was able to bring

00:46:19.080 --> 00:46:23.060
a perspective into all of the
conversations that we would

00:46:23.060 --> 00:46:27.560
have in terms of opportunities
to start to understand

00:46:27.560 --> 00:46:31.520
and reshape our culture.

00:46:31.520 --> 00:46:35.940
Because we had this family atmosphere when

00:46:35.940 --> 00:46:38.360
you would just hire people
and they would be just as much

00:46:38.360 --> 00:46:40.680
friends as they would be employees.

00:46:40.680 --> 00:46:45.680
And like you said, when
you get to about 20 people,

00:46:47.430 --> 00:46:51.510
we personally don't know what
every person's working on

00:46:53.210 --> 00:46:58.210
from day to day, we're
zoomed out from that.

00:46:58.730 --> 00:47:03.250
And we started to become
interested in instituting

00:47:03.250 --> 00:47:05.910
another level of accountability
where it's not just,

00:47:05.910 --> 00:47:08.140
hey, if we hire you, you're
a member of the family,

00:47:08.140 --> 00:47:13.140
no matter what, basically,
accountability was lax

00:47:14.550 --> 00:47:18.130
and there wasn't specific goals in mind.

00:47:18.130 --> 00:47:22.320
So there was a shift that Brian
really helped us lock into

00:47:22.320 --> 00:47:27.320
from moving from a family
culture to a team culture.

00:47:29.809 --> 00:47:32.573
And that can sound, just
wanted to acknowledge,

00:47:34.260 --> 00:47:36.040
that may sound, oh, that
sounds a little bit harsh,

00:47:36.040 --> 00:47:37.330
you mean it's not a family anymore?

00:47:37.330 --> 00:47:39.700
But I think that, this
was a helpful way for me

00:47:39.700 --> 00:47:42.340
to understand it because
I was initially resistant

00:47:42.340 --> 00:47:46.960
to the idea because we're
nice guys and we're actually

00:47:46.960 --> 00:47:51.960
pretty conflict avoidant and
we're very loyal and I think

00:47:52.820 --> 00:47:55.250
that those can be good
to a certain degree.

00:47:55.250 --> 00:47:57.720
But what we were finding is
that if you think about a family

00:47:57.720 --> 00:48:02.210
like, okay, here in my
family, especially as we're

00:48:02.210 --> 00:48:05.462
isolated here, it's like
everybody's expected to do things,

00:48:05.462 --> 00:48:07.800
the kids have their days
where they're supposed

00:48:07.800 --> 00:48:11.960
to clean their rooms or
unload the dishwasher

00:48:11.960 --> 00:48:13.740
and they don't always do that.

00:48:13.740 --> 00:48:15.920
No, they don't do that.
Does that mean...

00:48:15.920 --> 00:48:18.990
And even if one of my kids
decided that they were gonna

00:48:18.990 --> 00:48:22.053
refuse to ever do their part,

00:48:22.053 --> 00:48:23.790
will they no longer be my son?

00:48:23.790 --> 00:48:25.960
No, they're still gonna live
here and we'll get through it,

00:48:25.960 --> 00:48:27.190
they're still a part of the family.

00:48:27.190 --> 00:48:30.020
But if you run a business
like that, you run into

00:48:30.020 --> 00:48:31.870
some pretty obvious problems.

00:48:31.870 --> 00:48:34.850
But if you shift to a
team mentality, it's like,

00:48:34.850 --> 00:48:38.170
okay, when you think about
an athletic team, it's like,

00:48:38.170 --> 00:48:40.940
okay, the people who are on
the team are the ones who

00:48:40.940 --> 00:48:43.430
believe in the vision, are team players,

00:48:43.430 --> 00:48:46.080
but also can execute and do things well.

00:48:46.080 --> 00:48:48.490
It's like you get to be a
part of the starting five

00:48:48.490 --> 00:48:50.810
if you're one of the best
players and you get to be

00:48:50.810 --> 00:48:53.810
actually on the team if
you're a contributing player.

00:48:53.810 --> 00:48:58.090
And if you can't contribute
or if you just refuse,

00:48:58.090 --> 00:49:00.900
there's no birthright to
being a part of the team,

00:49:00.900 --> 00:49:03.500
it means that we might have
a difficult conversation

00:49:04.360 --> 00:49:06.460
and we might have the ultimate
difficult conversation

00:49:06.460 --> 00:49:08.740
which is to say we're
gonna have to part ways.

00:49:08.740 --> 00:49:12.460
And I think we learned that
there is such a thing as

00:49:12.460 --> 00:49:17.460
a cultural fit that not every
single person that we think

00:49:17.650 --> 00:49:20.040
will work will work.

00:49:20.040 --> 00:49:25.040
And just as often, that person
will be the one to discover

00:49:25.850 --> 00:49:29.880
what I'm actually interested
in and what I'm most gifted at

00:49:29.880 --> 00:49:33.210
and where I want to invest my
passions is not in alignment

00:49:33.210 --> 00:49:35.810
or it doesn't, it's not,
I'm not a puzzle piece

00:49:35.810 --> 00:49:37.123
that fits here.

00:49:38.220 --> 00:49:39.990
As we started to discover that,

00:49:39.990 --> 00:49:42.070
we started to set expectations.

00:49:42.070 --> 00:49:45.430
So when we would bring
someone new onto the team,

00:49:45.430 --> 00:49:48.987
we would say, "Okay, our
policy is there's basically

00:49:48.987 --> 00:49:50.537
"a six week trial.

00:49:50.537 --> 00:49:53.777
"At the six week mark, we're
gonna have an assessment

00:49:53.777 --> 00:49:56.683
"and it's gonna be a
conversation both ways,

00:49:58.127 --> 00:50:00.340
"just to know if this
is gonna work or not."

00:50:00.340 --> 00:50:03.680
Because you just can't hire somebody even

00:50:03.680 --> 00:50:08.400
in the most diligent way and
know if it's gonna work out.

00:50:08.400 --> 00:50:12.900
It's not reasonable, but I
think that we were bringing

00:50:12.900 --> 00:50:17.900
this mentality that, hey, if you're here,

00:50:18.040 --> 00:50:19.273
you're here for life.

00:50:20.597 --> 00:50:23.640
And we got that feedback from
some people that we brought on

00:50:23.640 --> 00:50:26.580
on a contract basis just
to work, or that's how

00:50:26.580 --> 00:50:29.690
they wanted to work,
especially in this town.

00:50:29.690 --> 00:50:32.560
So we learned from that and
I think we started to benefit

00:50:32.560 --> 00:50:36.476
from that expectation that six weeks down,

00:50:36.476 --> 00:50:38.930
we're gonna have this assessment to see

00:50:38.930 --> 00:50:41.510
if this is a good fit for everyone.

00:50:41.510 --> 00:50:46.510
Well, another piece of that
is this idea of very direct

00:50:46.910 --> 00:50:51.790
candid communication, and
continues to be a difficult thing

00:50:51.790 --> 00:50:53.820
for us to do just
naturally because we're not

00:50:53.820 --> 00:50:55.183
those kinds of bosses.

00:50:56.260 --> 00:51:00.730
But it's an important thing
again if you're the coach

00:51:00.730 --> 00:51:04.190
of a team and you're watching
your player do something

00:51:04.190 --> 00:51:07.020
or you're watching the tape
back, you wanna be like,

00:51:07.020 --> 00:51:08.720
hey, come here, take a look at this,

00:51:08.720 --> 00:51:09.730
you see what you did there?

00:51:09.730 --> 00:51:11.053
I'd rather you do this.

00:51:12.300 --> 00:51:14.290
That's not the way you
communicate with your family

00:51:14.290 --> 00:51:16.137
'cause if you do, if you try to,

00:51:16.137 --> 00:51:17.980
and I'm guilty of this
sometimes and I'm sure

00:51:17.980 --> 00:51:18.880
you are as well.

00:51:18.880 --> 00:51:23.440
Sometimes I'll go into business
leader mode in my family

00:51:23.440 --> 00:51:28.440
in trying to get my kids to
do things and it doesn't work

00:51:28.820 --> 00:51:30.660
in a family atmosphere
where everybody knows

00:51:30.660 --> 00:51:32.230
that they're gonna be here forever.

00:51:32.230 --> 00:51:34.700
But on the team atmosphere,
it's like, hey listen, no,

00:51:34.700 --> 00:51:38.060
this is actually your
compensation and your longevity

00:51:38.060 --> 00:51:40.943
at this company are based
on your contribution

00:51:40.943 --> 00:51:43.430
to the bottom line in a lot of ways,

00:51:43.430 --> 00:51:46.940
and so we're gonna have to have
some difficult conversations

00:51:46.940 --> 00:51:50.550
from time to time and
that that can become just

00:51:50.550 --> 00:51:53.863
an expectation and a part of your culture,

00:51:54.890 --> 00:51:58.250
the healthier everything's
gonna be, not easy to get there.

00:51:58.250 --> 00:52:01.930
I think we got into problems
when we wouldn't think

00:52:01.930 --> 00:52:03.980
someone was a good fit
or they wouldn't beat

00:52:03.980 --> 00:52:08.520
their performance, just
consistently wouldn't meet

00:52:10.330 --> 00:52:12.310
our standards or our goals,

00:52:12.310 --> 00:52:15.680
then it was hard to have
those conversations.

00:52:15.680 --> 00:52:19.430
But when we didn't have them
and we skirted the issue,

00:52:19.430 --> 00:52:21.073
it made everything worse.

00:52:21.940 --> 00:52:23.820
And then we're talking about partying ways

00:52:23.820 --> 00:52:27.290
and somebody feels blindsided
and sometimes you have to

00:52:27.290 --> 00:52:29.960
learn lessons, we had
to learn some lessons

00:52:29.960 --> 00:52:31.453
in a more painful way.

00:52:33.290 --> 00:52:38.290
And there's a right way to talk about it,

00:52:38.620 --> 00:52:42.880
but not talking about it is bad news.

00:52:42.880 --> 00:52:45.830
I think another thing that we
had to come to grips with was

00:52:48.730 --> 00:52:53.440
we couldn't just lead by osmosis of

00:52:53.440 --> 00:52:56.120
what our demeanor was and the
fact that we would show up

00:52:56.120 --> 00:53:01.120
as performers because people
would interact with us

00:53:01.650 --> 00:53:05.280
when we were performing,
shooting Good Mythical Morning

00:53:05.280 --> 00:53:06.343
and other things.

00:53:07.440 --> 00:53:10.540
But there was also an interaction
that we needed to have

00:53:10.540 --> 00:53:15.540
as leaders and being
visible as our company grew,

00:53:15.800 --> 00:53:18.430
especially to 80 people of,

00:53:18.430 --> 00:53:23.100
okay they are providing
leadership, I understand what this

00:53:23.100 --> 00:53:27.310
company is ultimately
interested in and made up to be

00:53:27.310 --> 00:53:29.740
and what is the culture
and what's the vibe and

00:53:31.300 --> 00:53:32.600
what does the company care about,

00:53:32.600 --> 00:53:35.040
because what do Rhett and Link care about?

00:53:35.040 --> 00:53:37.740
And do they care about me?

00:53:37.740 --> 00:53:42.620
All of those things led to
making practical decisions like

00:53:42.620 --> 00:53:46.200
having a company-wide meeting every month.

00:53:46.200 --> 00:53:49.320
That for month after month,
we would have to convince

00:53:49.320 --> 00:53:51.820
ourselves that we needed
to keep having it because

00:53:53.180 --> 00:53:55.820
we had to realize there
was value in everyone

00:53:55.820 --> 00:53:59.020
hearing from us and then
realizing there's things

00:53:59.020 --> 00:54:00.190
that we could bring to the table

00:54:00.190 --> 00:54:05.190
in terms of giving perspective
on here's an anecdote

00:54:05.930 --> 00:54:07.950
that we heard from a Mythical Beast.

00:54:07.950 --> 00:54:10.160
We're gonna share it with all
of you in order to help you

00:54:10.160 --> 00:54:14.220
understand the difference
that all of the work

00:54:14.220 --> 00:54:16.810
that we're doing makes in people's lives.

00:54:16.810 --> 00:54:19.730
Even if some people knew it
here or there, it's helpful

00:54:19.730 --> 00:54:23.850
for them to hear it from us
and just to hear and experience

00:54:23.850 --> 00:54:27.270
our passion for what we're working on.

00:54:27.270 --> 00:54:29.650
But also to have a little bit
of fun, I mean I don't know

00:54:29.650 --> 00:54:34.650
how we started opening every
meeting with people share

00:54:36.780 --> 00:54:40.680
if they've had a run in with
a celebrity, like a fight,

00:54:40.680 --> 00:54:43.300
like a lot of people who work for us,

00:54:43.300 --> 00:54:44.780
they get in brawls with celebrities.

00:54:44.780 --> 00:54:47.620
No, they just spot celebrities.

00:54:47.620 --> 00:54:48.700
So I wouldn't call that a running.

00:54:48.700 --> 00:54:53.370
I think somebody just told a
story at one point and then,

00:54:53.370 --> 00:54:55.190
yeah, now it's how we open every meeting

00:54:55.190 --> 00:54:57.100
and there's always,

00:54:57.100 --> 00:54:59.890
when you got a company that
size and you're in Los Angeles,

00:54:59.890 --> 00:55:02.630
without fail, someone has seen
a celebrity and it's almost

00:55:02.630 --> 00:55:06.580
always a funny interaction or a situation.

00:55:06.580 --> 00:55:07.630
And it's fun and it's connecting.

00:55:07.630 --> 00:55:11.800
And then we go through an agenda
of all the different areas

00:55:11.800 --> 00:55:16.430
and what milestones and
things that we're excited

00:55:16.430 --> 00:55:19.043
about night, and it took
a while for us to realize

00:55:19.043 --> 00:55:21.730
that there was value in that.

00:55:21.730 --> 00:55:23.730
It took us awhile to realize
there was value in it

00:55:23.730 --> 00:55:28.730
because again, there's been a
slow transition over the years

00:55:30.090 --> 00:55:35.080
of us seeing what we were
building at Mythical Entertainment

00:55:35.080 --> 00:55:38.040
as a vehicle to allow us to
do the things that we were

00:55:38.040 --> 00:55:39.540
most creatively excited about.

00:55:39.540 --> 00:55:42.510
Again, we didn't get in
this to be business people,

00:55:42.510 --> 00:55:45.460
we got in this to be creators.

00:55:45.460 --> 00:55:47.330
But then once you realize
you've got this business

00:55:47.330 --> 00:55:50.100
that needs to be led and
you have to be involved,

00:55:50.100 --> 00:55:52.190
sometimes you have to say,
okay, well I'm actually

00:55:52.190 --> 00:55:56.090
not going to be able to
just throw myself into this

00:55:56.090 --> 00:56:00.450
creative project and just be
the artist guy all the time

00:56:00.450 --> 00:56:03.080
that can come in when he wants to

00:56:03.080 --> 00:56:04.800
and just waits until he's inspired.

00:56:04.800 --> 00:56:07.470
It's like, no, you actually
have to provide the structure

00:56:07.470 --> 00:56:10.810
and the leadership for
a company to be healthy.

00:56:10.810 --> 00:56:15.810
And so those are two different things

00:56:15.870 --> 00:56:17.901
in two different modes.

00:56:17.901 --> 00:56:20.740
And so a lot of this just
comes down to scheduling for us

00:56:20.740 --> 00:56:22.540
and making sure that, okay,
now we've got the meeting,

00:56:22.540 --> 00:56:23.420
we gotta be ready for it.

00:56:23.420 --> 00:56:26.510
Do you think that when we
have our company wide meeting

00:56:26.510 --> 00:56:28.670
and there's the big boardroom table

00:56:28.670 --> 00:56:31.170
and everybody's around the
table and then in the bleachers

00:56:31.170 --> 00:56:35.020
behind the table and
here we are both seated

00:56:35.020 --> 00:56:40.020
next to each other, shoulder
to shoulder, behind the table

00:56:40.220 --> 00:56:45.220
facing them, not unlike, we
would sit behind the GMM desk

00:56:45.670 --> 00:56:49.720
and then there's like the
business banter version

00:56:49.720 --> 00:56:53.960
of Good Mythical Morning,
I wonder if they feel like

00:56:53.960 --> 00:56:56.690
it's a bit of a business show.

00:56:56.690 --> 00:57:01.610
Not that it's fake or that it's insincere.

00:57:01.610 --> 00:57:03.570
I don't think they would
think that, but just that it's

00:57:03.570 --> 00:57:05.833
a bit like the business version of GMM.

00:57:06.870 --> 00:57:10.960
I'm sure that it's a little
unusual for like an intern

00:57:10.960 --> 00:57:13.360
who has been watching the
show and then they come to the

00:57:13.360 --> 00:57:16.170
first meeting and, oh they're
just sitting at a desk

00:57:16.170 --> 00:57:17.730
like they do other shows.

00:57:17.730 --> 00:57:20.040
I've never really thought
about it, but yeah,

00:57:20.040 --> 00:57:22.030
but it's not as entertaining as the show.

00:57:22.030 --> 00:57:22.880
No.
For sure.

00:57:22.880 --> 00:57:23.713
No.

00:57:23.713 --> 00:57:26.400
And it doesn't need to be,
that's not what it's about.

00:57:26.400 --> 00:57:29.580
I'd say another aspect of this is that,

00:57:29.580 --> 00:57:32.330
so we're still learning a
bunch of lessons about that,

00:57:32.330 --> 00:57:35.900
about what it means to try to
be creative and try to lead

00:57:35.900 --> 00:57:38.543
a company and we have a lot to learn.

00:57:39.980 --> 00:57:44.810
But because we have sorted
out some of this and learned

00:57:44.810 --> 00:57:47.290
some of these lessons and put
some of these things in place

00:57:47.290 --> 00:57:49.430
and we felt like we had gotten
to a place where it was like

00:57:49.430 --> 00:57:51.440
there's a nice balance of
having creative freedom

00:57:51.440 --> 00:57:54.070
but being able to provide
leadership but also being able

00:57:54.070 --> 00:57:56.460
to entrust people to lead
teams really efficiently

00:57:56.460 --> 00:57:58.770
and to create a great product,

00:57:58.770 --> 00:58:03.750
that made the decision to
acquire Smosh last year

00:58:06.140 --> 00:58:08.240
an easier decision for us.

00:58:08.240 --> 00:58:10.680
Again, not something
we ever set out to do,

00:58:10.680 --> 00:58:12.380
we never thought we
were gonna be acquiring

00:58:12.380 --> 00:58:14.240
other internet businesses.

00:58:14.240 --> 00:58:17.090
And even if you had to ask us
at the beginning of the year

00:58:17.090 --> 00:58:18.700
that we did that, we would
have been like, you know what,

00:58:18.700 --> 00:58:20.330
that's not something we're interested in.

00:58:20.330 --> 00:58:22.390
It was serendipitous,
we told that story about

00:58:22.390 --> 00:58:24.600
how everything aligned up perfectly.

00:58:24.600 --> 00:58:29.600
But because we had a culture
and we had a system in place,

00:58:29.840 --> 00:58:33.920
it wasn't that big of a
stretch to incorporate

00:58:33.920 --> 00:58:38.920
a whole other team, a whole
other company into our company

00:58:41.100 --> 00:58:45.440
and apply a lot of the same
principles because we had gotten

00:58:45.440 --> 00:58:47.240
to a place where we were
comfortable with people

00:58:47.240 --> 00:58:48.720
who had their own creative vision.

00:58:48.720 --> 00:58:51.150
In fact, that was what we were after.

00:58:51.150 --> 00:58:53.800
And we just were like, but
here's the business structure

00:58:53.800 --> 00:58:56.990
and the production side of
things that you can put in place

00:58:56.990 --> 00:59:00.023
to make your content dreams come true,

00:59:01.670 --> 00:59:03.900
but you guys are the ones
that are gonna have to be

00:59:03.900 --> 00:59:05.080
in charge of the creative,

00:59:05.080 --> 00:59:07.950
you know your fan base,
you've got more subscribers

00:59:07.950 --> 00:59:11.030
than we do, you've been doing
this for longer than us.

00:59:11.030 --> 00:59:13.400
And that was an interesting
experiment of just being able

00:59:13.400 --> 00:59:16.420
to say, all right, let's take
the things that we've learned

00:59:16.420 --> 00:59:18.900
and apply those lessons
to this acquisition.

00:59:18.900 --> 00:59:22.260
But we've already got creatively motivated

00:59:22.260 --> 00:59:25.300
people in place and I think
that that's one of the reasons

00:59:25.300 --> 00:59:28.270
that it's worked so well for both sides.

00:59:28.270 --> 00:59:33.270
And I think hearing feedback
from Ian and from Smosh,

00:59:33.820 --> 00:59:37.370
it makes me feel good when
they talk about the contrast

00:59:37.370 --> 00:59:39.920
from their experience before to being

00:59:41.130 --> 00:59:46.130
under the wings of Mythical
because they describe it as

00:59:46.690 --> 00:59:49.793
a different experience being
in a creator led company.

00:59:50.652 --> 00:59:53.280
And I really think it comes down to,

00:59:53.280 --> 00:59:55.800
there's definitely business accountability

00:59:55.800 --> 01:00:00.800
and there's lots of structure
and there are of course

01:00:00.930 --> 01:00:03.090
corporate aspects to what we do now,

01:00:03.090 --> 01:00:05.220
with these many people, there has to be.

01:00:05.220 --> 01:00:10.220
But it's all built on the
foundation of belief that

01:00:12.450 --> 01:00:17.450
if you're passionately
expressing yourself in a way

01:00:17.570 --> 01:00:20.400
that's committed to reaching your audience

01:00:20.400 --> 01:00:24.810
and connecting with them
honestly, and you've got

01:00:24.810 --> 01:00:27.903
a proven track record,
that's earned trust.

01:00:33.190 --> 01:00:37.970
So the creative quotient is
a lot higher than in some

01:00:37.970 --> 01:00:40.930
companies where it's just, okay, we might,

01:00:40.930 --> 01:00:43.640
I don't know how other companies
would do it in terms of

01:00:43.640 --> 01:00:48.640
how they would calculate and
factor in creative decisions.

01:00:49.050 --> 01:00:52.390
But there's just a level
of trust and belief there,

01:00:52.390 --> 01:00:57.040
subject to parameters and
this doesn't just apply

01:00:57.040 --> 01:00:59.720
to Smosh, it applies to us
and we just convey it to them

01:00:59.720 --> 01:01:04.720
as well that that's what leads to success.

01:01:07.650 --> 01:01:09.640
You can't underestimate
the creative component

01:01:09.640 --> 01:01:11.473
and the passion involved in it.

01:01:12.538 --> 01:01:16.600
And the latest aspect of this,
recognizing that you have

01:01:16.600 --> 01:01:20.330
a culture, embracing that
culture and trying to shape

01:01:20.330 --> 01:01:24.540
that culture that's been
put into a bit of a test

01:01:24.540 --> 01:01:27.440
with what's happened with the coronavirus

01:01:27.440 --> 01:01:31.960
and we're very much as you
can tell by the fact that

01:01:31.960 --> 01:01:36.060
we're at home, we're obeying
the social distancing

01:01:36.060 --> 01:01:38.500
requirements and no one
is working at the office,

01:01:38.500 --> 01:01:40.130
everybody's working from home.

01:01:40.130 --> 01:01:43.150
And that's created its
own unique challenges from

01:01:43.150 --> 01:01:46.160
a technical perspective,
which we've overcome,

01:01:46.160 --> 01:01:50.070
but it's been a unique
challenge in terms of

01:01:50.070 --> 01:01:54.160
the cultural aspect of the
company because what is the

01:01:54.160 --> 01:01:57.170
culture now when everyone
is just at home all day

01:01:57.170 --> 01:01:59.683
and communicating via video conference?

01:02:00.730 --> 01:02:04.220
So I think one of the things
that we decided to do early on

01:02:04.220 --> 01:02:06.710
is like, well let's have
our company-wide meeting,

01:02:06.710 --> 01:02:10.330
but let's do it as a big
Zoom call and everybody's

01:02:10.330 --> 01:02:15.330
gonna be on there, and it was
interestingly or surprisingly

01:02:17.640 --> 01:02:20.950
connecting and we got a lot
of feedback from people.

01:02:20.950 --> 01:02:23.950
We did the thing that we
always do, we started it off

01:02:23.950 --> 01:02:28.020
asking people to tell
celebrity spotting stories.

01:02:28.020 --> 01:02:32.333
There was actually one, somebody
saw John Ham on a balcony,

01:02:33.870 --> 01:02:36.680
and it's like it's a sort
of a different flavor

01:02:36.680 --> 01:02:39.060
when we're all social distancing.

01:02:39.060 --> 01:02:42.920
But what we realize is that
having made the decision

01:02:42.920 --> 01:02:47.870
to set up this regular touch
point where we're communicating

01:02:47.870 --> 01:02:49.700
with our company and our
company is communicating

01:02:49.700 --> 01:02:53.440
with each other, it wasn't a
difficult thing to carry that

01:02:53.440 --> 01:02:56.510
into this weird time,
that we're just talking

01:02:56.510 --> 01:02:57.343
on video conference.

01:02:57.343 --> 01:02:58.400
But there was an added
weight to it just coming

01:02:58.400 --> 01:03:00.610
to the realization that
this was a leadership moment

01:03:00.610 --> 01:03:05.360
where we could communicate
what the plan was

01:03:05.360 --> 01:03:09.200
and give as much as we could,
a sense of security like

01:03:09.200 --> 01:03:12.100
okay, we are moving forward.

01:03:12.100 --> 01:03:14.580
This is a difficult and trying time

01:03:14.580 --> 01:03:16.263
but we're in this together.

01:03:17.700 --> 01:03:19.940
So yeah, there was, it's like

01:03:23.140 --> 01:03:25.350
I feel needed as a leader,
I feel like this is

01:03:25.350 --> 01:03:30.330
an opportunity for us and it's not easy.

01:03:30.330 --> 01:03:33.390
I think when you look at
this scenario or looking back

01:03:33.390 --> 01:03:34.870
on everything, there was never a point

01:03:34.870 --> 01:03:38.853
where we felt fully ready.

01:03:40.680 --> 01:03:45.380
Saying that we were blindsided
by the CEO ness of it all

01:03:45.380 --> 01:03:49.720
is something that you
just, you take every day

01:03:49.720 --> 01:03:52.450
and you take every
opportunity for what it is

01:03:52.450 --> 01:03:55.330
and we learn from our mistakes and

01:03:55.330 --> 01:03:57.060
we try to learn from everything.

01:03:57.060 --> 01:04:00.810
I think these lessons
again are still for us

01:04:00.810 --> 01:04:03.470
as we're continuing to
learn and hone those and...

01:04:05.504 --> 01:04:08.590
I don't know, especially in
light of what we're dealing with

01:04:08.590 --> 01:04:12.140
now, I just have this added perspective of

01:04:12.140 --> 01:04:16.620
it's a privilege to be
able to lead a company

01:04:16.620 --> 01:04:18.700
even though I never
knew I wanted to do that

01:04:18.700 --> 01:04:20.750
and sometimes it's still very frustrating

01:04:20.750 --> 01:04:23.180
and it doesn't seem like the
main thing that I wanna be

01:04:23.180 --> 01:04:27.710
working on, I do have this sense that

01:04:30.320 --> 01:04:31.920
it's not only a weighty obligation,

01:04:31.920 --> 01:04:33.880
but it's a privilege to do that

01:04:33.880 --> 01:04:38.110
and to shape a culture and a
company that not only cares

01:04:38.110 --> 01:04:41.043
about what we're creating,
but it cares about,

01:04:42.200 --> 01:04:46.160
and we care about all the
people that we're working with.

01:04:46.160 --> 01:04:51.160
And as certainly, I'm as
grateful as I've ever been

01:04:52.900 --> 01:04:55.410
for everybody who works for us,

01:04:55.410 --> 01:04:57.900
given what we're going through right now.

01:04:57.900 --> 01:04:59.980
And I think that's a
good way to wrap it up

01:04:59.980 --> 01:05:02.403
with one final lesson, and that is,

01:05:04.050 --> 01:05:09.050
we're having to learn to always
be looking for the lesson

01:05:10.100 --> 01:05:11.460
that we're currently learning.

01:05:11.460 --> 01:05:15.780
A lot of these lessons are
things that you don't see them

01:05:15.780 --> 01:05:19.790
until you look back until
there's some perspective

01:05:19.790 --> 01:05:20.913
after the fact.

01:05:21.990 --> 01:05:25.570
But I think that over time
you get a little bit better

01:05:25.570 --> 01:05:27.610
at being like, oh,
actually, there's a lesson

01:05:27.610 --> 01:05:28.443
happening right now.

01:05:28.443 --> 01:05:31.510
If I can actually figure out
what it is and anticipate it

01:05:31.510 --> 01:05:34.260
and learn it right now, then
we can begin to apply it.

01:05:34.260 --> 01:05:37.680
And I think that this
crisis and this change,

01:05:37.680 --> 01:05:39.900
the way that our company
has been forced to adapt,

01:05:39.900 --> 01:05:41.350
we're learning new lessons.

01:05:41.350 --> 01:05:44.170
The big thing that we've been trying to do

01:05:46.380 --> 01:05:49.590
in more recent years is figuring
out what does Mythical mean

01:05:49.590 --> 01:05:51.960
beyond just the two of us?

01:05:51.960 --> 01:05:54.130
What does it mean in terms of content?

01:05:54.130 --> 01:05:57.330
What does it mean in
terms of just the company

01:05:57.330 --> 01:05:58.230
and the leadership?

01:05:58.230 --> 01:06:02.010
And there's only so much that
the two of us can accomplish

01:06:02.010 --> 01:06:05.890
as leaders and as creators,
but the concept of Mythical,

01:06:05.890 --> 01:06:09.690
thanks to our incredible team and also to

01:06:09.690 --> 01:06:12.590
the incredible fans who have
bought into what we're doing.

01:06:13.440 --> 01:06:16.210
It's given us, we're learning
this lesson that hold on,

01:06:16.210 --> 01:06:18.380
whether we want it to be or not,

01:06:18.380 --> 01:06:20.460
this is already bigger than us.

01:06:20.460 --> 01:06:24.140
But how do you manage that
wisely and how do you continue

01:06:24.140 --> 01:06:27.520
to grow it in a way that it doesn't lose

01:06:27.520 --> 01:06:30.970
what makes Mythical Mythical
and continues to give us

01:06:30.970 --> 01:06:34.840
opportunities to, we still want
to personally create things

01:06:34.840 --> 01:06:36.270
that are awesome.

01:06:36.270 --> 01:06:38.900
We're not done creating new things.

01:06:38.900 --> 01:06:41.500
It's not like we're about
to walk off into the sunset

01:06:41.500 --> 01:06:44.733
and say, hey, this is what
we've made and now we're done,

01:06:45.730 --> 01:06:48.000
we still, and we probably
always will think this way,

01:06:48.000 --> 01:06:50.200
we still think our best
work is ahead of us.

01:06:51.360 --> 01:06:55.640
But also, we've been forced
to embrace the fact that

01:06:55.640 --> 01:07:00.010
oh, this Mythical thing is
just happened and it has

01:07:00.010 --> 01:07:05.010
a lot of potential, let's do
this wisely, let's deliver.

01:07:05.180 --> 01:07:10.180
I think our best work now
that we come to grips with it

01:07:11.170 --> 01:07:16.170
also includes prioritizing
not only our own health,

01:07:16.350 --> 01:07:20.710
but the health of our team,
that they're not just employees,

01:07:20.710 --> 01:07:21.543
but they're people.

01:07:21.543 --> 01:07:24.360
And if creating a
culture is something that

01:07:26.170 --> 01:07:28.040
I am increasingly passionate about

01:07:29.670 --> 01:07:33.590
because it's a great opportunity

01:07:33.590 --> 01:07:38.060
and also to continue to make
things that make a difference.

01:07:38.060 --> 01:07:39.890
It's not just making
this stuff we wanna make,

01:07:39.890 --> 01:07:43.180
but making it in a way that
continues to make a difference

01:07:43.180 --> 01:07:48.150
in people's lives and like
you said, continuing to learn

01:07:49.300 --> 01:07:53.470
lessons along the way from
not only our successes

01:07:53.470 --> 01:07:55.350
but our shortcomings.

01:07:55.350 --> 01:08:00.180
So it's an amazing position
that we find ourselves in

01:08:00.180 --> 01:08:02.960
and I'm very grateful for it.

01:08:02.960 --> 01:08:03.793
Very.

01:08:03.793 --> 01:08:06.220
You got any other lessons
or you just have a wreck?

01:08:06.220 --> 01:08:07.233
Oh, yeah.

01:08:08.450 --> 01:08:12.230
Well just to get very practical,
at the top of the year,

01:08:12.230 --> 01:08:15.197
we took all of our team
leaders and we said,

01:08:15.197 --> 01:08:17.737
"Let's go through a book
together that we can discuss

01:08:17.737 --> 01:08:21.060
"in how we can all be better leaders."

01:08:21.060 --> 01:08:23.183
And so I recommend that book to you,

01:08:23.183 --> 01:08:27.910
it's called "The Making of
a Manager" by Julie Zhuo,

01:08:27.910 --> 01:08:30.923
last name is spelled Z-H-U-O.

01:08:32.968 --> 01:08:35.790
If you're interested in
tackling some of those issues

01:08:35.790 --> 01:08:40.790
about how to inspire the
